window.__registerChapter({"num": 10, "slug": "010_Heritable_Disorders_of_Connective_Tissue", "title": "Heritable Disorders of Connective Tissue", "authors": "Fransiska Lieve Malfait", "part_num": "II", "part_title": "Developmental Structural Disorders", "word_count": 27367, "pearls": ["The exact role of 3-hydroxyproline residues is unknown, though their presence confers specific biomechanical properties and influences collagen fiber assembly; a severe autosomal recessive form of osteogenesis imperfecta is related to pathogenic variants in P3H1 .", "Most if not all HCTDs show full penetrance but are characterized by variable expression , wherein varying degrees of severity are seen in different individuals, including members of the same family that have the same pathogenic variant.", "Since HCTDs are genetically heterogeneous, prevalence ranges from rather commonly occurring autosomal dominant disorders to extremely rare recessively inherited conditions.", "Pathogenic variants in genes coding for the fibrillar collagens type III and V and, to a lesser extent, type I are found in many individuals with autosomal dominantinherited forms of EDS; in the rare autosomal recessive forms of EDS, pathogenic variants in other genes are at fault.", "Classical EDS and vascular EDS are two of the more common autosomal dominant forms of EDS.", "Biallelic COL1A2 pathogenic variants that result in a complete absence of α2 chains in type I collagen give rise to the very rare autosomal recessive cardiac-valvular EDS .", "Musculocontractural EDS results from pathogenic variants in genes encoding enzymes responsible for dermatan biosynthesis: CHST14 , dermatan 4-O-sulfotransferase 1, and DSE , dermatan sulfate epimerase.", "In more severe types, such as the autosomal recessive musculocontractural and classical-like EDS, a bleeding diathesis may manifest itself as severe spontaneous or recurring hematomas."], "genes": ["AAA1", "AAA2", "AAA3", "AAA4", "ABCC9", "ACAN", "ACG2", "ACTA2", "ADAMTS2", "ADAMTSL4", "ADCL3", "AEBP1", "ALDH18A1", "ANFH1", "ANTXR1", "ARCL2", "ARCL2B", "ARCL3A"], "omims": ["#100070", "#104200", "#108300", "#112264", "#113811", "#114000", "#120070", "#120090", "#120110", "#120120", "#120130", "#120131", "#120140", "#120150"], "subtopics": ["FRANSISKA LIEVE MALFAIT", "Collagens", "Collagen Structure and Classification", "Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d", "Collagen Biosynthesis", "Microfibrils and Elastic Fibers", "Proteoglycans", "Glycoproteins", "Heritable Connective Tissue Disorders", "The Ehlers-Danlos Syndromes", "Molecular Basis", "Clinical Manifestations", "Skin", "Joints"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0196_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.1", "caption": "Biosynthesis of a “prototype” collagen. The procollagen α-chains are synthesized by ribosomes in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Already during the synthesis of the nascent polypeptide, certain prolyl and lysyl residues are hydroxylated and modified by glycosylation. Three α-chains associate to form a trimer and fold into a triple helix. This process is facilitated by heat shock protein (HSP) 47, a collagen-specific molecular chaperone in the ER. The transport and Golgi organization (TANGO) protein 1 mediates secretion of the newly formed triple helical procollagen into the extracellular space, where the N- and C-terminal propeptides are cleaved by specific proteases. The mature collagen molecules assemble to form mixed fibrils with other collagens and non-collagenous molecules. The suprastructures are stabilized by covalent crosslinks. Small leucine-rich proteoglycans (SLRPs) and other proteoglycans have roles in the regulation of collagen fibrillogenesis and crosslinking. EDS, Ehlers–Danlos syndrome."}, {"img": "fig_p0200_06.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.3", "caption": "Clinical features of Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (EDS). Patients with classical EDS demonstrating hyperextensible skin (A), joint hypermobility at the wrist (B), atrophic horseshoe-shaped scars on the shin (C), piezogenic papules on the heel (D), and the Gorlin sign (E). (F) Translucent skin with visible veins on the chest in a patient with vascular EDS."}, {"img": "fig_p0206_11.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.4", "caption": "Atrophic scarring in an individual with classical EhlersDanlos syndrome."}, {"img": "fig_p0207_06.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.5", "caption": "Beighton score. The range of motion of several key small and large joints is measured to provide an overview of joint hypermobility. Instability is not assessed. Scoring: 2 points for each bilateral measure in Nos. 1 to 4 and 1 point for No. 5, equaling a total possible score of 9. Hypermobility is considered significant with a score of ≥6 between the ages of 6 and 35. (Modified from Smits-Engelsman B, Klerks M, Kirby A. Beighton score: a valid measure for generalized hypermobility in children. J Pediatr . 2011;158:119-123.e4.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0208_07.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.6", "caption": "Keratoconus. (A) Unaffected eye, (B) eye with keratoconus-related conical protrusion, (C) comparison of unaffected cornea and cornea with keratoconus."}, {"img": "fig_p0209_07.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.7", "caption": "Illustration of the clinical spectrum of findings in individuals with vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome secondary to pathogenic variants in COL3A1."}, {"img": "fig_p0210_11.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.8", "caption": "Schematic representation of fibrillin-1 gene and protein. FBN1 consists of 65 exons located on chromosome 15. It encodes fibrillin-1 (2,871 amino acids, 350 kDa), a multi-domain glycoprotein, integral for ECM stability."}, {"img": "fig_p0211_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.8", "caption": "Schematic representation of fibrillin-1 gene and protein. FBN1 consists of 65 exons located on chromosome 15. It encodes fibrillin-1 (2,871 amino acids, 350 kDa), a multi-domain glycoprotein, integral for ECM stability."}, {"img": "fig_p0211_10.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.9", "caption": "Schematic representation of extracellular matrix disintegration in Marfan syndrome. Fibrillin-1 aggregates into functional microfibrils and sequesters TGF-β in the ECM, regulating its signaling. Loss of fibrillin-1 in Marfan syndrome decreases or eliminates this TGF-β sequestration, and its constitutive stimulation activates genes like MMPs, which induce elastolysis, decreasing ECM stability."}, {"img": "fig_p0212_05.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.10", "caption": "Marfan syndrome. Note the long slim limbs, pectus excavatum, narrow face, and reduced elbow extension."}, {"img": "fig_p0213_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.11", "caption": "Marfan syndrome. Note the joint laxity (A), Steinberg thumb sign (B), ability to join thumb and fifth finger around the wrist (Walker-Murdoch sign) (C), pes planus (D), and striae over hips and back (E)."}, {"img": "fig_p0221_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.12", "caption": "Histochemical and electron microscopic analysis of elastic fibers in cutis laxa (CL). Hart elastin (e) stain of skin sections from healthy donor (A), individual with autosomal dominant CL-I (ADCL-I) and elastin pathogenic variant (B), individual with autosomal recessive CL-I (ARCL-IA) and fibulin-5 pathogenic variant (C), individual with ARCL-IB and fibulin-4 pathogenic variants (D), and individual with ARCL-IC and LTBP4 variant (E). Arrowheads indicate elaunin fibers in papillary dermis, and arrows show elastic fibers in reticular dermis. Note paucity of elaunin fibers in CL skin (B to E) and either underdeveloped (B and D) or globular (C and E) elastin deposits in reticular dermis. Electron microscopic analysis of elastic fibers from healthy donor (F), individual with ADCL and elastin pathogenic variant (G), patient with ARCL-IA and fibulin-5 variant (H), and patient with ARCLIC and LTBP4 variant (I). Note the close association of microfibrils (mf) with elastin in the control and elastin deposits devoid of microfibrils in all 3 CL samples. col, Collagen fibrils. Magnification bars: 50 μm (A to D), 20 μm (E), and 200 nm (F to I). (From Berk DR, Bentley DD, Bayliss SJ, Lind A, Urban Z. Cutis laxa: a review. J Am Acad Dermatol . 2012;66(5):842.e1-842.e17.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0222_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.13", "caption": "Manifestations of autosomal recessive cutis laxa type I. (A) Redundant skin and large ears in an individual with fibulin-5 pathogenic variant. (B) Sagging cheeks and periorbital area, reverse-V eyebrows, long philtrum, and large ears in an individual with fibulin-4 pathogenic variant. (C) Redundant skin and joint laxity on the legs of an individual with ARCL-I. Computed tomography images of tortuous aorta (arrows) and diaphragmatic hernia (arrowhead) in an individual with fibulin-4 variant (D) and aortic aneurysm (arrow) in an individual with fibulin-4 variant (E)."}, {"img": "fig_p0223_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.14", "caption": "Cranial CT scan showing occipital horns."}, {"img": "fig_p0223_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.15", "caption": "Manifestations of autosomal dominant cutis laxa. (A) Photograph of affected individual at age 13 years. (B) Chest radiograph of a 51-year-old individual with severe emphysema shown by bilateral hyperexpanded lung fields. (C) Magnetic resonance imaging of aortic root in an affected individual shows a 5.8 cm aneurysm (white bar)."}, {"img": "fig_p0224_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 10.16", "caption": "Generalized cutis laxa in a child with LTBP4 -related cutis laxa syndrome."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 10.1", "title": "#### 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations*"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.2", "title": "#### 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.1", "title": "Hereditary Collagen Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—co nt’d"}, {"label": "Table 10.4", "title": "Disorder Collagen Type Genetic Alteration Major Clinical Features"}, {"label": "Table 10.5", "title": "Disorder Collagen Type Genetic Alteration Major Clinical Features"}, {"label": "Table 10.6", "title": "Disorder Collagen Type Genetic Alteration Major Clinical Features"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.1", "title": "#### Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.8", "title": "##### 10.1 #### Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.1", "title": "#### Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.10", "title": "#### 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d"}, {"label": "Table 10.11", "title": "Disorder Collagen Type Genetic Alteration Major Clinical Features"}, {"label": "Table 10.12", "title": "Disorder Collagen Type Genetic Alteration Major Clinical Features"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.2", "title": "Hereditary i Disorders Associated to Reduced Syn i thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific i Collagen Types"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.2", "title": "Hereditary i Disorders Associated to Reduced Syn i thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific C i ollagen Types—cont’d"}, {"label": "Table 10.15", "title": "Hereditary D i TABLE 10.2 isorders Associated to Reduced Sy i nthesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific i Collagen Ty"}, {"label": "Table 10.16", "title": "Hereditary D i TABLE 10.2 isorders Associated to Reduced Syn i thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific i Collagen Ty"}, {"label": "Table 10.17", "title": "TABLE No n-exhausti ve List of Genetic Diseases Cause d by Defects in Core Connective Tissue Components and a"}, {"label": "Table 10.18", "title": "TABLE Non-exhaustive List of Genetic Diseases Caused by Defects in Core Connective Tissue Components and a"}, {"label": "Table 10.19", "title": "Protein Gene(s) Disease Key Manifestations"}, {"label": "Table 10.20", "title": "Clinical Hallmarks"}, {"label": "Table 10.21", "title": "EDS Type (Abbreviation) Gene (Protein) Major Clinical Criteria Clinical Hallmarks Minor Clinical Criteria"}, {"label": "Table 10.22", "title": "Clinical Hallmarks"}, {"label": "Table 10.23", "title": "EDS Type (Abbreviation) Gene (Protein) Major Clinical Criteria Clinical Hallmarks Minor Clinical Criteria"}, {"label": "Table 10.24", "title": "EDS Type (Abbreviation) Gene (Protein) Major Clinical Criteria Clinical Hallmarks Minor Clinical Criteria"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.25", "title": "##### 10.5 ### The Differential Diagnosis of Marfan Syndrome"}, {"label": "Table 10.26", "title": "• Urticaria and/or angioedema"}, {"label": "Table 10.27", "title": "Disorder Inheritance Gene Protein Distribution of Cutis Laxa Additional Features"}, {"label": "Table 10.28", "title": "Disorder Inheritance Gene Protein Distribution of Cutis Laxa Additional Features"}, {"label": "Table 10.29", "title": "Disorder Inheritance Gene Protein Distribution of Cutis Laxa Additional Features"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.8", "title": "Additional Syndromes Featuring La x or Wrinkled Skin"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.9", "title": "The Sti ckler Syndromes and Al lied Collagenopathies"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.32", "title": "### 10.10 Diagnostic Criteria for Marfan Syndrome"}, {"label": "TABLE 10.12", "title": "Thoracic Aortic Ane urysm Syndromes and Conditions Du e to a Heritable or Genetic Cause"}, {"label": "Table 10.34", "title": "Condition Gene Clinical Features"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.176 -->\n\n# 10 Heritable Disorders of Connective Tissue \n\n#### **FRANSISKA LIEVE MALFAIT** \n\nConnective tissue is the most abundant and widely distributed tissue in the body, serving as the supportive framework for the vertebrate body and as an integral component of every organ system, having a crucial role in organ function. Connective tissue consists of relatively few cells embedded in an intricate three-dimensional network of macromolecules called the **extracellular matrix (ECM)** . The ECM is a dynamic environment with vital roles in both health and disease, facilitating cell adhesion, migration, and differentiation, as well as cell-cell and cellmatrix communication. \n\nThe complexity of the ECM is reflected in the sheer number of its core and associated proteins, collectively termed the **matrisome** . The _core matrisome_ consists of approximately 300 proteins divided into 3 major categories: **collagens** , **proteoglycans** , and **glycoproteins** . These molecules are composed of repeated characteristic structures, such as collagen domains, thrombospondin motifs, and epidermal growth factor domains, arranged in a modular fashion that varies depending on the ultrastructural needs of the underlying tissue. This organization allows for the formation of highly structured yet versatile supramolecular assemblies that facilitate interaction within and between molecules. These interactions confer diverse characteristics to their respective tissues, such as the resilience of tendon and cartilage, the hardened composition of bone, the transparency of the cornea, and the filtration capacity of the kidney. Extracellular matrix functions are mostly derived from this supramolecular structure and are much less a function of the molecular properties of the _individual_ components. Matrisome-associated proteins consist of ECM-affiliated proteins, secreted proteins, and ECM regulators such as degrading and crosslinking enzymes. Hereditary connective tissue disorders (HCTDs) are related primarily to defects in the structure or function of the core matrisome, as well as of elastin and microfibrillar proteins, and are less often related to defects in matrisome-associated proteins. \n\n## **Collagens** \n\nThe collagen protein superfamily consists of at least 28 types of collagens, over 40 distinct polypeptides, and more than 20 proteins with collagenous domains. Collagens are the most abundant proteins in the animal kingdom, providing structural strength to the ECM and promoting cell adhesion, migration, angiogenesis, and tissue repair. Their fundamental nature is exemplified by the number of rare genetic diseases associated with collagen defects (Tables 10.1, 10.2, and 10.3). \n\n### **Collagen Structure and Classification** \n\nThe characteristic feature of collagen is the presence of at least one triple helical collagenous (COL) domain. The COL domain is a right-handed supercoil consisting of three identical (i.e., homotrimer) or non-identical (i.e., heterotrimer) left-handed α chains. These chains are rich in Gly-Xaa-Yaa triplet repeats, in which glycine is the only amino acid small enough to reside at the inside of the tightly-wound supercoil. The triple helix is further stabilized by interchain hydrogen bonds, electrostatic interactions involving lysine and aspartate, and a high content of proline and 4-hydroxyproline (4Hyp) at the X and Y positions, respectively. The COL domain can be flanked by and interspersed with several non-collagenous domains, such as amino- (N-) and carboxy- (C-) pro-domains, as well as fibronectin type III, Kunitz-type domains, thrombospondin-1, and von Willebrand domains. These various non-collagenous domains confer specific binding affinities and structural features through interactions with other ECM molecules. \n\n**Fibril-forming** or **fibrillar collagens** make up approximately 70% of all collagen in the human body. These rod-like structures are flanked by N- and C-terminal non-collagenous domains or pro-peptides and consist of an uninterrupted triple helical domain assembled into 280 nm long, highly \n\n_(Text continued on page 173)_\n\n<!-- p.177 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n#### **10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations*** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Ehlers-Danlos<br>syndrome (EDS)|**COL1**<br>**COL3**<br>**COL5**|Variants in 20 genes have been found to cause EDS<br>(#130000).<br>Classical EDS (#130010) results from variants in either the<br>_COL5A1_(*120215) or_COL5A2_(*120190) gene. Variants<br>in_COL1A1_(*120150) or_COL1A2_(*120160) that lead to<br>skipping of exon 6 of the mRNA coding the pro α1 or<br>pro α2-chain result in EDS arthrochalasia type (#130060;<br>#617821). Inheritance is autosomal dominant.<br>Biallelic variants in_COL1A2_that lead to a<br>complete absence of pro α2-chains result in cardiac-<br>valvular EDS (#225320), a very rare autosomal recessive<br>type of EDS.|EDS is the name associated with at least thirteen phenotypically characterized<br>clinical entities, which result from different types of variants in distinct collagen<br>genes and several collagen processing genes. These disorders are biochemically<br>and clinically distinct although they all manifest structural weakness in<br>connective tissue as a result of defects in the structure and function of<br>collagens. Although all types of EDS affect joints and skin, additional features<br>vary by type. Severity ranges from mild to severe. Joint hypermobility occurs<br>with most forms of EDS. Infants with hypermobile joints often have weak muscle<br>tone, which can delay the development of motor skills such as sitting, standing,<br>and walking. The loose joints are unstable and prone to dislocation and chronic<br>pain. Many EDS patients have soft, velvety skin that is highly stretchy (skin<br>hyperextensibility) and fragile. Affected individuals tend to bruise easily and in<br>some cases, they show atrophic scars. People with the classical form of EDS<br>experience wounds that split open with little bleeding and leave scars that widen<br>over time to create characteristic “cigarette paper” scars.|\n|||Variants in the_COL3A1_(*120150) are associated with the<br>vascular type of EDS (#130050).|The vascular type can involve serious and potentially life-threatening complications<br>due to unpredictable tearing of blood vessels. This rupture can cause internal<br>bleeding, stroke and shock. The EDS vascular type is also associated with an<br>increased risk of organ rupture, including tearing of the intestine or the uterus<br>(womb) during pregnancy.|\n|||Variants in procollagen-lysine, 2-oxoglutarate 5-dioxygenase<br>1 gene (_PLOD1_, *153454) and FK506 binding protein 14<br>(_FKBP14_, *614505) are responsible for the EDS<br>kyphoscoliotic type I and II (EDSKSCL, #225400;<br>#614557). Inheritance is autosomal recessive in both<br>cases. PLOD1 catalyzes the hydroxylation of lysyl residues<br>in collagen-like peptides, which are critical for the stability<br>of intermolecular crosslinks. FKBP14 acts at the level of<br>the protein folding in the ER, including components of the<br>ECM (COL1, COL3, COL6, and fibronectin).|In addition to the classical symptoms of EDS, patients with kEDS-_PLOG1_and<br>kEDS-_FKBP14_are characterized also by progressive kyphoscoliosis with muscle<br>hypotonia from birth, joint laxity, gross motor delay, severe skin hyperextensibility,<br>easy bruising, fragility of sclerae, myopathy, and hearing loss.|\n|||The Musculocontractural Type I and type II form of EDS<br>(EDSMC1, #601776; EDSMC2, #615539) are caused by<br>recessive loss-of-function variants in the carbohydrate<br>sulfotransferase 14 (_CHST14_, *608429) and in the<br>dermatan sulphate epimerase (_DSE_, *605942) genes,<br>respectively. The genes encode enzymes involved in the<br>dermatan sulfate (DS) biosynthesis that is involved in the<br>assembly of collagen fibril.|EDSMC1 and 2 share most of the clinical features, even though the majority of<br>cases (31) refer to the EDSMC1 and only three cases are reported for the<br>EDSMC2 type. The two syndromes are characterized by progressive<br>kyphoscoliosis, adducted thumbs in infancy or clenched fists and talipes<br>equinovarus, hands with atypically shallow palmar creases and tapering fingers,<br>joint hypermobility, clubfoot, arachnodactyly, elastic skin, and poor wound<br>healing. Craniofacial features include brachycephaly, large fontanelle,<br>hypertelorism, downslanting palpebral fissures, microcorneae, strabismus,<br>prominent nasolabial folds, short philtrum, thin upper lip, small mouth, high<br>palate, and microretrognathia. EDSMC neonates show distal arthrogryposis and<br>muscular hypotonia.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.178 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n#### **10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|||The Spondylodysplastic Type 1 (also known as progeroid<br>form of EDS) and 2 forms of EDS (EDSSPD1, #130070;<br>EDSSPD2, #615349) are caused by variants in the β-1,4-<br>Galactosyltransferase 7 gene (_B4GALT7_, *604327) and<br>β-1,3-Galactosyltransferase 6 (_B3GALT6_, *615291),<br>respectively. The genes encode enzymes involved in the<br>biosynthesis of the proteoglycans.<br>The spondylodysplastic type 3 form of EDS (EDSSPD3)<br>results from variants in the membrane-bound zinc<br>transporter_SLC39A13_(*608735) and shows clinical<br>overlap with EDSSPD1-2. Variants in_SLC39A13_result<br>in increased Zn<sup>2+</sup>content inside the endoplasmic<br>reticulum, which inhibits the proper collagen crosslinking<br>and the stability of the collagen triple helix.<br>These three conditions have an autosomal recessive<br>inheritance.|Patients with EDSSPD1-2 showed short stature, muscle hypotonia, radioulnar<br>synostosis, and mild intellectual disability (ID). In addition, they present facial<br>dysmorphism, hyperextensible skin, joint hypermobility (JHM), single transverse<br>palmar crease, severe hypermetropia, limb bowing, and osteopenia.|\n|||The dermatosparaxis type of EDS (EDSDERMS, #225410)<br>results from variants in disintegrin and metalloproteinase<br>with thrombospondin motifs (_ADAMTS2_, *604539), the<br>gene encoding the procollagen peptidase that cleavages<br>the N-propeptide of the fibrillar procollagens types I–III<br>and V. This condition has an autosomal recessive<br>inheritance.|The EDSDERMS is characterized by skin that sags and wrinkles. Extra (redundant)<br>folds of skin may be present as affected children get older.|\n|Osteogenesis<br>Imperfecta (OI)|**COL1**|Variants in the_COL1A1_(*120150) and_COL1A2_(*120160)<br>genes are responsible for more than 90% of all cases of<br>OI (#166200). Most of the variants causative of OI type I<br>affect_COL1A1_gene and result in reduced levels of<br>COL1, whereas those responsible for most of OI types II<br>(#166210), III (#259420) and IV (#166220) cases occur in<br>_COL1A1_or_COL1A2_genes and impair COL1 structure.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|At least four biochemically and clinically distinguishable forms of OI have been<br>identified associated to defects in COL1. These are named as OI type I (mild),<br>type II (perinatal lethal), type III (deforming) and type IV (mild deforming). A defect<br>in COL1 structure weakens connective tissues, particularly bones. All four forms<br>of OI present reduced levels of COL1 and brittle bones that break easily. Multiple<br>fractures result in bone deformities. Additional symptoms may include blue<br>sclera, short height, loose joints, hearing loss, breathing and teeth problems,<br>cervical artery dissection, and aortic dissection.|\n|||Variants in Cartilage associated protein_CRTAP_, *605497),<br>Prolyl 3-hydroxylase (_P3H1_, *610339) and Peptidyl-prolyl<br>isomerase B (_PPIB_, *123841) genes results in OI type VII<br>(#610682), VIII (#610915) and IX (#259440), respectively.<br>CRTAP encodes a cartilage-associated protein whereas<br>P3H1 an enzyme belonging to the collagen propyl<br>hydroxylase family. PPIB encodes for a cyclophilins<br>(Cyps) protein that catalyzes the cis–trans isomerization<br>of peptide bonds.<br>All these proteins are required for proper collagen<br>synthesis, assembly and secretion. In these cases, the<br>inheritance is autosomal recessive.|In addition to the four forms of OI previously described, eleven additional<br>phenotypically related disorders in the OI family exist, all associated with bone<br>fragility and low bone mass. Among the OI associated to collagen alterations,<br>the type VII (variants in_CRTAP_) is sometimes considered a lethal form with<br>multiple fractures, long bone deformities, scoliosis, and short stature. The type<br>VIII form of OI (variants in_P3H1_) includes severe growth defects, skeletal<br>demineralization, scoliosis, round face, and proptosis.<br>The type IX (variants in_PPIB_) is a very severe form of OI. Embryos dye during<br>pregnancy or few months after birth. Radiographs and an autopsy showed the<br>presence of shortened, bowed, and fractured long bones without evident<br>rhizomelia.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.179 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.1** Hereditary Collagen Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—co nt’d\n\n|Disorder|Type|Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|||The Serpin family H member 1 (SERPINH1, *600943) gene<br>encodes a collagen-binding protein that has chaperone<br>activity in the endoplasmic reticulum. Variants in<br>_SERPINH1_cause the type X OI (#613848), whose<br>inheritance is autosomal recessive.|Types X OI is a severe deforming form of the disorder characterized by aberrant<br>collagen crosslinking, folding, and chaperoning.|\n|||Absence of FK506 binding protein 10 (_FKBP10_, *607063)<br>in recessive type XI OI (#610968) leads to reduced<br>collagen cross-linking and deposition. FKBP10 encodes<br>a chaperone that contributes to type I procollagen<br>folding. Variants in this gene affect its secretion.|Clinical hallmarks of OI type XI are congenital contractures. All the others clinical<br>data on the 29 patients with OI type XI (variants in_FKBP10_) are limited and<br>heterogeneous regarding the age of onset, the number of fractures, the type of<br>affected bones, and the severity of the disorder.|\n|||The recessive form of OI type XIII (OI13, #614856) is caused<br>by variants in the_BMP1_(*112264) gene, which is<br>involved in the processing of the C-propeptides of<br>procollagens types I–III and the proteolytic activation of<br>the enzyme lysyl oxidase, necessary for the formation of<br>covalent cross-links in collagen and elastic fibers.|<br>The OI type XIII is characterized by normal teeth, faint blue sclerae, severe growth<br>deficiency, borderline osteoporosis, and an average of 10–15 fractures a year<br>affecting both the upper and lower limbs and with severe bone deformity.|\n|||The Secreted protein acidic and cysteine rich (_SPARC_,<br>*182120) gene encodes a glycoprotein that binds to<br>COL1 and other ECM proteins.<br>Variants in this gene are responsible for the type XVII OI<br>(#616507) and seem to result in the over-modification of<br>collagen during triple-helical formation.<br>The inheritance is autosomal recessive.|Two clinical cases have been reported: the first is a girl from North Africa with low<br>bone mineral density (BMD), scoliosis, short stature, mild joint hyperlaxity, weak<br>underdeveloped muscles of the lower extremities, bowing of both humeri, and<br>speech delay. The second patient is an Indian girl, who had a left hip dislocation<br>at the age of 10 weeks, muscle hypotonia, and gross motor developmental<br>delay. Other features are decreased calf muscle mass, joint hyperlaxity, and soft<br>skin.|\n|Caffey disease, also<br>called infantile<br>cortical<br>hyperostosis|**COL1**|Two missense variants in_COL1A1_(*120150) (variant<br>c.3040C>T, p.[Arg836Cys] and variant c. c.2752C>T,<br>p.[Arg918Cys]) have been identified in individuals with<br>Caffey disease (#114000). Inheritance is autosomal<br>dominant but not all people who inherit the variant<br>develop signs and symptoms. The amino acid change<br>leads to COL1 fibrils that are variable in size and shape.|Caffey disease is characterized by excessive new bone formation (hyperostosis) in<br>early infants. Affected bones may double or triple in width and include jawbone,<br>scapulae, clavicles, and the shafts (diaphyses) of long bones in arms and leg.<br>Affected babies are frequently feverish and irritable. They show swelling of joints,<br>pain, and redness of affected areas. Usually, there is spontaneous resolution of<br>the inflammatory signs within few months or years. Rare cases of recurrence<br>have also been described.|\n|Spondyloepiphysea l<br>dysplasia<br>congenita (SED)|**COL2**|SED congenita (#183900) is caused by heterozygous<br>variant in_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on chromosome<br>12q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|SED congenita is a chondrodysplasia characterized by short spine, barrel-shaped<br>chest, abnormal epiphyses, and flattened vertebral bodies. Skeletal features are<br>manifested at birth and evolve with time. Other features include myopia and/or<br>retinal degeneration with retinal detachment and cleft palate.|\n|Stanescu type of<br>spondyloepiphyseal<br>dysplasia (SEDSTN)|<br> <br>**COL2**|SEDSTN (#616583) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on<br>chromosome 12q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with accumulation of glycoprotein in chondrocytes has<br>been designated the “Stanescu type.” Clinical hallmarks include progressive joint<br>contracture with premature degenerative joint disease, particularly in the knee, hip,<br>and finger joints and swollen interphalangeal joints of the hands. The affected<br>individuals are not short, despite the presence of a short trunk. Radiologically,<br>spondylar and epiphyseal abnormalities are quite conspicuous. Other clinical<br>characteristics are generalized platyspondyly, hypoplastic pelvis, epiphyseal flattening<br>with metaphyseal splaying of the long bones, and enlarged phalangeal epimetaphyses<br>of the hands.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.180 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Multiple epiphyseal<br>dysplasia with<br>myopia and<br>conductive<br>deafness (EDMMD)|**COL2**|EDMMD (#132450) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on<br>chromosome 12q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|EDMMD is characterized by epiphyseal dysplasia associated with progressive<br>myopia, retinal thinning, crenated cataracts, conductive deafness, joint pain,<br>deformity, waddling gait, and short stature. In 1978 Beighton and colleagues<br>described an Afrikaner family in South Africa in which the mother, two sons, and<br>one daughter had a syndrome of multiple epiphyseal dysplasia, myopia, and<br>conductive deafness. The patients had short stature, brachydactyly, genu valgus<br>deformity, and dysplasia of the epiphyses. The epiphyses around the knee joint<br>were flattened, the femoral necks were widened, and the vertebral bodies were<br>mildly reduced in height and were concave on their upper and lower surfaces.|\n|Achondrogenesis<br>type II (ACG2)|**COL2**|ACG2 (#200610) is caused by variants in_COL2A1_gene<br>(*120140) on chromosome 12q13. The inheritance is<br>autosomal dominant but somatic and germline mosaicism<br>have also been reported.|ACG2 is characterized by severe micromelic dwarfism with small chest and<br>prominent abdomen. Other clinical features include incomplete bone ossification<br>and disorganization of the costochondral junction. The cartilage appears as<br>abnormal gelatinous texture and translucent.|\n|Czech dysplasia|**COL2**|Czech dysplasia (#609162) is caused by heterozygous<br>variants in_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on chromosome<br>12q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Czech dysplasia is a skeletal dysplasia characterized by early and progressive<br>onset, shortening of the third and fourth toes caused by metatarsal hypoplasia.<br>Affected individuals have a normal stature but usually complain of severe joint<br>pain before adolescence. Clinical signs are restricted mobility in the lower limb<br>joints and kyphoscoliosis. Skeletal radiographs reveal signs of<br>pseudorheumatoid. Narrow joint spaces and flattened epiphyses platyspondyly<br>with irregular endplates and elongated vertebrae can be observed in the most<br>severe cases. Only five affected families from the Czech Republic have been so<br>far reported.|\n|Legg-Calve-Perthes<br>disease (LCPD)|**COL2**|LCPD (#150600) is caused by heterozygous variant in the<br>_COL2A1_(*120140) gene on chromosome 12q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|LCPD is a form of avascular necrosis of the femoral head (ANFH; #608805) that<br>affects hip development in growing children. It is due to loss of circulation in the<br>femoral head. Radiology does not permit an early diagnosis that depends on the<br>phase of disease progression through ischemia, revascularization, fracture and<br>collapse, repair and remodeling of the bone. LCPD affects more often boys who<br>are usually shorter than their peers.|\n|Osteoarthritis with<br>mild<br>chondrodysplasia<br>(OSCDP)|**COL2**|OSCDP (#604864) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on chromosome 12q13. The<br>inheritance is autosomal dominant.|OSCDP is a common disease that produces joint pain and stiffness together with<br>radiologic evidence of progressive degeneration of joint cartilage. Several cases<br>have been reported, including family members over various generations. Major<br>features are primary osteoarthritis associated with mild chondrodysplasia. Over<br>the years the range of motion becomes limited. In about 60% of affected<br>persons, abnormalities of the vertebral bodies consistent with mild<br>chondrodysplasia have been described, including platyspondyly, irregular end<br>plates, herniations within the vertebral bodies (Schmorl nodes), and anterior<br>wedging. Other minor changes include iliac exostoses.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.181 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Torrance type of<br>platyspondylic<br>lethal skeletal<br>dysplasia (PLSD-T)|**COL2**|PLSD-T (#151210) can be caused by heterozygous variant<br>in_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on chromosome 12q13. The<br>disease is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner.<br>All the patients analyzed so far have variants in the C-<br>propeptide domain of_COL2A1_, which lead to altered<br>biosynthesis. The phenotype could result from a<br>combination of diminished collagen fibril formation, toxic<br>effects through the accumulation of unfolded collagen<br>chains inside the chondrocytes and/or alteration of a<br>putative signaling function of the C-propeptide.|PLSD-T is a rare skeletal dysplasia characterized by platyspondyly, brachydactyly,<br>and metaphyseal changes. Radiology reveals decreased ossification of the skull<br>base, short thin ribs, hypoplastic pelvis with wide sacrosciatic notches and flat<br>acetabular roof, short tubular long bones with ragged metaphyses, and bowing<br>of the radius. Histologically, the growth plate appeared relatively normal. The<br>resting cartilage appeared hypercellular with large chondrocytes. Though<br>generally lethal in the perinatal period, a few long-term survivors with PLSD-T<br>have been reported. Some patients also present shortening of long bones,<br>degenerative changes in the proximal femora, limited elbow extension, midface<br>hypoplasia, myopia, deafness, and mental retardation.|\n|Strudwick type of<br>spondyloepimeta-<br>physeal dysplasia<br>(SEMD)|**COL2**|SEMD (#184250) is an autosomal dominant disorder<br>caused by heterozygous variant in_COL2A1_gene<br>(*120140) on chromosome 12q13.|SEMD clinical features include severe dwarfism, marked pectus carinatum, and<br>scoliosis. Cleft palate and retinal detachment are frequently associated.<br>Distinctive radiographic feature is irregular sclerotic changes, described as<br>“dappled” in the metaphyses of the long bones that are caused by alternating<br>zones of osteopenia and osteosclerosis.|\n|Spondyloperipheral<br>dysplasia|**COL2**|Spondyloperipheral dysplasia (#271700) is autosomal<br>dominant disorder caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL2A1_gene (*120140) on chromosome 12q13.|The disorder is a skeletal dysplasia with platyspondyly and brachydactyly E-like<br>changes (short metacarpals and metatarsals, short distal phalanges in the hand<br>and feet).|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>(STL)|**COL2**<br>**COL9**<br>**COL11**|Pathogenic variants in one of six genes (_COL2A1_,<br>_COL11A1_,_COL11A2_,_COL9A1_,_COL9A2_, and_COL9A3_)<br>can be associated with Stickler syndrome.<br>STL is inherited in autosomal dominant manner when<br>altered in_COL2A1_,_COL11A1_, or_COL11A2_, in<br>autosomal recessive manner when altered in_COL9A1_,<br>_COL9A2_, or_COL9A3_.|STL is a genetically heterogeneous connective tissue disorder characterized by<br>myopia, cataract and retinal detachment, conductive and sensorineural hearing<br>loss. Additional findings may include mid–facial underdevelopment and cleft<br>palate, mild spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, and/or precocious arthritis. Variable<br>phenotypic expression occurs within and among families. Interfamilial variability<br>is partially explained by locus and allelic heterogeneity.|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>type I (STL1)||STL1 (#108300), also called the membranous vitreous<br>type, is caused by heterozygous variant in_COL2A1_<br>gene (*120140) on chromosome 12q13.|STL1 patients usually display a congenital vitreous abnormality consisting of a<br>vestigial gel in the retrolental space, bounded by a highly folded membrane.<br>Most affected individuals are at high risk for retinal detachment. Systemic<br>features typically seen in STL1 are premature osteoarthritis, cleft palate, hearing<br>impairment, and craniofacial abnormalities.|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>type II (STL2)||STL2 (#604841), sometimes called the beaded vitreous<br>type, is caused by heterozygous variant in_COL11A1_<br>gene (*120280) on chromosome 1p21.|Patients affected by STL2 are myopic, rarely with paravascular lattice retinopathy.<br>They frequently present cataract or are aphakic or pseudophakic. Retinal<br>detachment, either mono-or bi-lateral may appear in the third decade.<br>Moreover,_COL11A1_variants are associated by early-onset hearing loss.|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>type III (STL3)||STL3 (#184840) or “nonocular Stickler syndrome” has been<br>recently reclassified as form of otospondylomegaepiphyseal<br>dysplasia or Weissenbacher-Zweymuller syndrome<br>(OSMEDA or WZS). It is caused by heterozygous variants in<br>_COL11A2_gene (*120290) on 6p21 chromosome.|Patients affected by STL3 have typical facial features, including midface hypoplasia<br>combined with hearing impairment. No ocular abnormalities are reported. They<br>present relatively short extremities with abnormally large knees and elbows but<br>normal total body length. Diagnostic radiologic findings are enlarged epiphyses<br>combined with moderate platyspondyly, mainly in the lower thoracic region.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.182 -->\n\n**TABLE 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>type IV (STL4)||STL4 (#614134) is caused by homozygous variant in<br>_COL9A1_gene (*120210) on chromosome 6q13.|Individuals affected by STL4 have moderate-to-severe sensorineural hearing loss,<br>moderate-to-high myopia with vitreoretinopathy, cataracts and epiphyseal<br>dysplasia. The vitreous abnormality resembles an aged vitreous rather than the<br>typical membranous, beaded or non-fibrillar type.|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>type V (STL5)||STL5 (#614284) is caused by homozygous variant in<br>_COL9A2_gene (*120260) on chromosome 1p34.|One family with STL5 has been reported. Major clinical findings are high myopia,<br>vitreoretinal degeneration, retinal detachment, hearing loss and short stature.<br>None of the family members was known to have cleft palate, and although there<br>was short stature in childhood, normal height was found in adults.|\n|Stickler syndrome<br>atypical||The atypical form of STL (#609508) with predominantly<br>ocular findings is caused by variant in_COL2A1_gene<br>(*120140). The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Patients display high myopia and retinal detachment. Systemic features of<br>premature osteoarthritis, cleft palate, hearing impairment and craniofacial<br>abnormalities are very mild or absent.|\n|Familial avascular<br>necrosis of the<br>femoral head-1<br>(ANFH1)|**COL2**|ANFH1 (#608805) is an autosomal dominant disorder<br>caused by heterozygous variant in_COL2A1_gene<br>(*120140) on chromosome 12q13.|ANFH1 is a debilitating disease affecting young adults between 35 and 55 years of<br>age. The disorder is characterized by progressive pain in the groin, mechanical<br>failure of the subchondral bone, and degeneration of the hip joint. Nearly half of<br>patients require hip replacement before 40 years of age.|\n|Kniest dysplasia|**COL2**|Kniest dysplasia (#156550) is caused by variants in<br>_COL2A1_gene (*120140). The inheritance is autosomal<br>dominant.|Patients have short stature, flat facial profile, high myopia, risk of retinal<br>detachment, cleft palate, deafness, high risk of severe degenerative joint<br>disease, and odontoid hypoplasia, leading to risk of atlantoaxial instability and<br>paralysis. Other features include neonatal respiratory distress, infantile hypotonia,<br>abnormal oval-shaped vertebra at birth and later platyspondyly, shortened,<br>“dumbbell-shaped” long bones, with splaying of the epiphyses and metaphyses.|\n|Alport syndrome|**COL4**|Alport syndrome is a clinically and genetically<br>heterogeneous nephropathy. Approximately 80% of<br>cases are transmitted as an X-linked semi-dominant<br>condition due to_COL4A5_variants. 20% of cases are<br>autosomal recessive due to variant in either_COL4A3_or<br>_COL4A4_.<br>Same families with autosomal-dominant Alport syndrome<br>have been reported, either caused by_COL4A3_or<br>_COL4A4_variants.|Alport syndrome is characterized by progressive nephritis associated with hearing<br>loss and sometime ocular lesions. Patients experience progressive loss of kidney<br>function. The majority of affected individuals have blood (hematuria) and high<br>levels of proteins (proteinuria) in their urine, which indicate impaired kidney<br>function. Many patients also develop hypertension and at end-stage renal<br>disease. Ocular anomalies are frequent in Alport syndrome, and they can<br>precede proteinuria in 40% of patients. Anterior lenticonus, abnormal coloration<br>of the retina, lens rupture, cataracts, and corneal erosions can be found.<br>Pregnancy of patients with Alport syndrome is very challenging and often<br>|\n|Alport syndrome<br>autosomal<br>dominant||The autosomal dominant form of Alport syndrome<br>(#104200) is caused by heterozygous variant in_COL4A3_<br>gene (*120070).|complicated by deterioration of renal function, preeclampsia, severe placental<br>dysfunction, and sometime acute renal failure. Preterm delivery is frequent.|\n|Alport syndrome<br>X-LINKED (ATS)||ATS (#301050) is caused by variants in_COL4A5_(*303630)<br>gene. The inheritance is dominant.|ATS males are more severely affected than females. Men have a 90% chance of<br>developing end-stage kidney disease by age 40. Patients with large deletions or<br>nonsense variants have significantly earlier onset than those with missense<br>variants. The majority (95.5%) of women with_COL4A5_variants develop<br>microscopic hematuria.|\n|Leiomyomatosis,<br>diffuse, with Alport<br>syndrome (DL-ATS)||DL-ATS (#308940) is caused by large deletions involving<br>_COL4A5_(*303630) and COL4A6 (*303631) genes. Likely<br>an X-linked semi-dominant inheritance.|DL-ATS reveals the Alport syndrome features associated with diffuse<br>leiomyomatosis.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.183 -->\n\n**TABLE 10.1** \n\n#### **Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Alport syndrome<br>autosomal<br>recessive||This form of Alport syndrome (#203780) is caused by<br>variants in_COL4A3_(*120070) or_COL4A4_(*120131)<br>gene.|Autosomal recessive Alport syndrome presents as gross proteinuria in childhood<br>and progression to end-stage kidney disease often before the fourth decade.|\n|Autosomal dominant<br>mental<br>retardation-34<br>(MRD34)|**COL4**|MRD34 (#616351) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL4A3BP_(*604677) gene on chromosome 5q13.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Patients with MRD34 present unremarkable perinatal history and delivery with a<br>normal birth weight. Neonatal feeding difficulties may occur. Psychomotor<br>development is delayed and speech skills limited. Auto-mutilation behavior and<br>anxiety are observed. Normal growth parameters and no evident dysmorphism<br>are recorded in adults.|\n|Retinal arterial<br>tortuosity (RATOR)|**COL4**|RATOR (#180000) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL4A1_gene (*120130) on chromosome 13q34. The<br>inheritance is autosomal dominant.<br>One single family with approximately 20 familial cases has<br>been reported so far.|RATOR is an uncommon condition characterized by marked tortuosity of second-and<br>third-order retinal arteries with normal first-order arteries and venous system. Typically,<br>the vascular tortuosity is predominantly located at the macular and peripapillary area<br>and develops during childhood or early adulthood. Although the disease may be<br>asymptomatic, most patients complain of variable degrees of transient vision loss<br>due to retinal hemorrhage following physical exertion or minor trauma. Involvement of<br>non-ocular vascular beds has not been demonstrated in most cases but occasionally<br>other associated vascular abnormalities have been recorded, including malformations<br>in the Kieselbach nasal septum, spinal cord vascular mass, telangiectasis of bulbar<br>conjunctiva, and internal carotid artery aneurysm.|\n|Hereditary angiopathy<br>with nephropathy,<br>aneurysms and<br>muscle cramps<br>(HANAC)|<br>**COL4**|HANAC (#611773) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL4A1_gene (*120130) on chromosome 13q34.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|HANAC syndrome is characterized by angiopathy that affects several parts of the<br>body. Patients present kidney alterations consisting of multiple renal cysts and<br>sometimes hematuria. The brain is only mildly affected and intracranial<br>aneurysms causing hemorrhagic stroke can occur. Leukoencephalopathy is<br>found in about half of affected individuals whereas muscle cramps are<br>experienced by most patients in early childhood. In addition, patients may<br>manifest eye problems, like arterial retinal tortuosity, cataract and abnormality<br>called Axenfeld-Rieger anomaly.|\n|Small vessel disease<br>of the brain with or<br>without ocular<br>anomalies (BSVD)|**COL4**|BSVD (#607595) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL4A1_gene (*120130) on chromosome 13q34.<br>The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|BSVD is characterized by a wide spectrum of symptoms of varying severity<br>including porencephaly variably associated with eye defects (retinal arterial<br>tortuosity, Axenfeld-Rieger anomaly, cataract) and systemic findings such as<br>kidney involvement, muscle cramps, cerebral aneurysms, Raynaud<br>phenomenon, cardiac arrhythmia, and hemolytic anemia. Stroke is often the first<br>symptom and is usually caused by hemorrhagic rather than ischemic stroke.<br>Patients also have leukoencephalopathy and may experience infantile<br>hemiparesis, seizures and migraine headaches accompanied by visual auras.|\n|Porencephaly|**COL4**|Porencephaly is an autosomal dominant disorder<br>characterize by variants in_COL4A1_(*120130) or<br>_COL4A2_(*120090) genes on chromosome 13q34.|It is a neurological disorder characterized by fluid-filled cysts or cavities in the brain<br>and is thought to result from disturbed vascular supply leading to cerebral<br>degeneration. Affected individuals have delayed growth and development,<br>hypotonia, spastic hemiplegia, seizures, migraine headaches, speech problems,<br>and intellectual disability with variable severity.|\n|Porencephaly-1<br>(POREN1)||POREN1 (#175780) is caused by variants in_COL4A1_gene.|POREN1 is more common. It is usually unilateral and results from destructive<br>lesions.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.184 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n##### **10.1** \n\n#### **Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Porencephaly-2<br>(POREN2)||POREN2 (#614483) is caused by variants in_COL4A2_<br>gene.|POREN2 is usually symmetrical and results from developmental malformation.|\n|Schizencephaly|**COL4**|Some patients with schizencephaly (#269160) have<br>variants in_COL4A1_(*120130) gene.|Schizencephaly is a very rare cortical malformation that results in gray matter line<br>clefts impacting one or both sides of the brain. Two types of schizencephaly<br>have been described, depending on the size of the area involved and on the<br>separation of the cleft lips. The clinical picture is mainly based on the presence<br>of motor deficits and mental retardation but the severity of the symptoms varies<br>depending on the size and location of the clefts and on the presence of<br>associated cerebral malformations. Patients with type I are almost normal, they<br>may have seizures or motor impairment. Type II is associated with mental<br>retardation, seizures, hypotonia, spasticity, inability to walk or speak, and<br>blindness.|\n|Susceptibility to<br>intracerebral<br>hemorrhage (ICH)|**COL4**|ICH (#614519) may be due to variants in_COL4A2_<br>(*120090) or_COL4A1_(*120130) genes on chromosome<br>13q34. The inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Few patients with adult-onset hemorrhagic stroke have been reported. The<br>pathogenic gene variant vascular collagen diminishes the tensile strength of<br>vessels and increases their fragility, which can lead to hemorrhage.|\n|X-linked deafness-6<br>(DFNX6)|**COL4**|DFNX6 (#300914) is caused by variant in_COL4A6_gene<br>(*303631) on chromosome Xq22. One family has been<br>reported so far.|The symptoms vary in male and female patients affected by this disorder. The<br>severe bilateral sensorineural hearing loss apparent in infancy affects only males,<br>who present bilateral malformation of the cochlea with incomplete separation<br>from the internal auditory canal. Language skills in these patients are severely<br>restricted. Female patients develop mild to moderate hearing impairment in the<br>third/fourth decades of life and rarely hearing loss in the first decade of life.|\n|Benign familial<br>haematuria (BFH)|**COL4**|BFH (#141200) are caused by variants in_COL4A3_<br>(*120070) or_COL4A4_(*120131) gene, both of which<br>map on chromosome 2q36. The inheritance is<br>autosomal dominant.|BFH is characterized by the presence of persistent or recurrent hematuria, usually<br>detected in childhood. Hematuria remains isolated and never results in end-<br>stage renal disease. Diffuse attenuation of the glomerular basement membrane<br>is usually considered the hallmark of the condition but it is not specific.|\n|Bethlem myopathy-1<br>(BTHLM1)|**COL6**|BTHLM1 (#158810) is caused by variants in_COL6A1_<br>(*120220),_COL6A2_(*120240), or_COL6A3_(*120250)<br>genes, giving rise to the altered or even lack of type VI<br>collagen. Both recessive and dominant variants have<br>been reported.|The disease is characterized by progressive muscle weakness and joint stiffness<br>(contractures). The features can appear at any age, in some cases before birth<br>(decreased fetal movements) in other cases during infancy with joint laxity (loose<br>joints) and hypotonia (weak muscle tone). Later, during childhood, patients<br>develop contractures in their fingers, wrists, elbows, and ankles. When adult,<br>they may develop weakness in respiratory muscles, which result in breathing<br>difficulty. The mild form may also reveal skin abnormalities, including follicular<br>hyperkeratosis on the arms and legs; soft, velvety skin on the hand palms and<br>feet soles; abnormal wound healing resulting in shallow scars.|\n|Ullrich congenital<br>muscular<br>dystrophy-1<br>(UCMD1)|**COL6**|UCMD1 (#254090) is caused by variants in_COL6A1_<br>(*120220),_COL6A2_(*120240) or_COL6A3_(*120250)<br>genes, giving rise to the altered or even lack of type VI<br>collagen.<br>The disease is transmitted in an autosomal recessive<br>manner and only in rare cases in a dominant pattern.|Patients suffer from a severe muscle weakness beginning soon after birth. Some<br>affected individuals are never able to walk and others can walk only with support.<br>Several lose ambulation ability in adolescence. Progressive scoliosis and<br>deterioration of respiratory function is a typical feature. Some patients need<br>continuous mechanical ventilation to help them breathing. Affected individuals<br>develop contractures in their neck, hips and knees, which further impair movement.<br>There may be joint laxity in patient fingers, wrists, toes, ankles, and other joints. As<br>in BTHLM1, some people with UCMD1 have follicular hyperkeratosis.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.185 -->\n\n**TABLE 10.1** \n\n#### **Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Autosomal recessive<br>myosclerosis|**COL6**|The autosomal recessive myosclerosis (#255600) has an<br>autosomal recessive inheritance and is caused by<br>variants in_COL6A2_gene (*120240). One family has been<br>reported so far.|<br>The disorder is characterized by chronic inflammation of skeletal muscle with hyperplasia<br>of the interstitial connective tissue. The clinical symptoms include slender muscles with<br>“woody” consistency and restriction of movement of many joints because of muscle<br>contractures. Muscles are thin and may result sclerotic on palpation. The few patients<br>so far described showed difficulty in running and climbing stairs and had Achilles<br>tendon contractures during early childhood. Skeletal muscle biopsies showed a<br>myopathic pattern with fibrosis, proliferation of endomysial and perimysial connective<br>tissue, variation of myofiber diameter. Increased serum creatine kinase was also<br>found.|\n|Dystonia 27 (DYT27)|**COL6**|DYT27 (#616411) is caused by compound heterozygous<br>variants in_COL6A3_gene (*120250) on chromosome<br>2q37.<br>It is an autosomal recessive disorder.|Neurological disorder characterized by the onset of segmental isolated dystonia<br>involving the face, neck, bulbar muscles, and upper limbs in the first two decades<br>of life. Few cases have been reported and the symptoms included dystonic action<br>and postural tremor, writer’s cramp, oromandibular and laryngeal dystonia.|\n|The dystrophic forms<br>of epidermolysis<br>bullosa (DEB)|**COL7**<br>**COL17**|The autosomal dominant form of epidermolysis bullosa<br>dystrophica (DDEB, #131750) is caused by heterozygous<br>variants in_COL7A1_gene (*120120) on chromosome<br>3p21.<br>The autosomal recessive dystrophic form of epidermolysis<br>bullosa (RDEB, #226600) and the_RDEB_localized variant<br>(#226650) are caused by homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variants in_COL17A1_gene (*113811).|<br> <br>Epidermolysis bullosa (EB) is a term referring to a family of disorders that are<br>associated with excessive blistering in response to mechanical injury or trauma.<br>Microscopic examination of the skin shows cleavage below the basement<br>membrane within the papillary dermis. The signs and symptoms of this condition<br>vary widely among affected individuals. In mild cases, blistering may primarily<br>affect the hands, feet, knees and elbows. Severe cases involve widespread<br>blistering leading to vision loss, disfigurement, and other serious medical<br>problems such as strictures of the gastrointestinal tract leading to poor nutrition.<br>Patients show an increased risk of developing aggressive squamous cell<br>carcinoma. Kids with EB are often defined “butterfly wing” children because of<br>their extremely fragile skin, which can shed at the slightest touch.<br>DEB is one of the major forms of EB. DDEB and RDEB are also known as<br>Cockayne-Touraine disease and Hallopeau-Siemens disease, respectively.<br>Variations in severity are observed among the different forms of RDEB. Notably,<br>a functional SNP in MMP1 (*120353) promoter is associated with high severity in<br>RDEB. Since COL7 is degraded by MMP1, an imbalance between COL7<br>synthesis and degradation could worsen the RDEB phenotype.|\n|Nonsyndromic<br>congenital nail<br>disorder-8<br>(NDNC8)|**COL7**|NDNC8 (#607523) is caused by heterozygous variants in<br>_COL7A1_gene (*120120) on chromosome 3p21.1. The<br>disorder is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner.|This form of isolated toenail dystrophy has been found in few Japanese families in<br>which other members had the autosomal recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa<br>(RDEB, #226600) or the transient bullous dermolysis of the newborn (#131705), the<br>features of which include dystrophic nails. The nail plates of the toes were buried in<br>the nail bed and the free edge of the toenail was deformed and narrow.|\n|Fuchs endothelial<br>corneal<br>dystrophy-1<br>(FECD1)|**COL8**|FECD1 (#136800) is caused by heterozygous variants in<br>_COL8A2_gene (*120252) on chromosome 1p34.<br>It is an autosomal dominant disorder.|FECD is a progressive, bilateral condition leading to reduced vision quality due to<br>dysfunction of the corneal endothelial cells, a thin layer of cells in the back of the<br>cornea that regulates the amount of fluid inside the cornea. FECD occurs when the<br>endothelial cells die and the cornea becomes swollen with too much fluid. Corneal<br>endothelial cells continue to die over time, resulting in further vision problems.<br>Ultrastructural features include loss and attenuation of endothelial cells with<br>thickening and excrescences (guttae) of the underlying basement membrane that<br>are the clinical hallmark of FECD and that worsen with disease progression. As the<br>endothelial layer develops confluent guttae in the central cornea, the cornea<br>becomes dehydrated and clear. In the USA about 5% of the over 40 population is<br>affected by FECD and some early-onset cases are due to_COL8A2_variants.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.186 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n#### **10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Posterior<br>polymorphous<br>corneal dystrophy<br>(PPCD2)|**COL8**|A single family with PPCD2 (#609140) caused by<br>heterozygous missense variant in_COL8A2_gene (*120252)<br>has been described. Another family with one PPCD2<br>patient and few FECD cases, due to heterozygous<br>missense variant in_COL8A2_, has been described [139].|Father and daughter with PPCD2 have been reported. The patients show a<br>bilateral penetrating keratoplasty at the age of 20s (daughter) and 50s (father).<br>The authors suggested that the underlying pathogenesis of FECD and PPCD2<br>may be related to disturbance of the role of COL8 in influencing the terminal<br>differentiation of the neural crest-derived corneal endothelial cell.|\n|Multiple epiphyseal<br>dysplasia (EDM)|**COL9**|There are two types of EDM, which can be distinguished by<br>their pattern of inheritance, the dominant and recessive<br>types. EDM caused by variants affecting collagen<br>structures have an autosomal dominant transmission.<br>Variants in_COL9A1_,_COL9A2_or_COL9A3_genes are<br>found in less than 5% of individuals with dominant EDM.|EDM is a clinically and genetically heterogeneous skeletal disorder, which is<br>characterized by joint pain and stiffness, mild short stature and degenerative joint<br>features. Both cartilage and bone development are affected, mainly at the ends of<br>the long bones in the arms and legs (epiphyses). It has been suggested that<br>variants in COL9A1,_COL9A2_, or_COL9A3_genes may cause COL9 to accumulate<br>inside the cell or interact abnormally with other cartilage components.|\n|Multiple epiphyseal<br>dysplasia-2 (EDM2)||EDM2 (#600204) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL9A2_gene (*120260) on chromosome 1p34.|EDM2 onset is usually in childhood, around 3–4 years of age and clinical variability<br>is observed even within the same family.|\n|Multiple epiphyseal<br>dysplasia-3 (EDM3)||EDM3 (#600969) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL9A3_gene (*120270).|EDM3 patients show early-onset short stature, waddling gait and pain/stiffness in<br>the knees. Few patients experience involvement of elbow, wrist or ankle.|\n|Multiple epiphyseal<br>dysplasia-6 (EDM6)||EDM6 (#614135) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL9A1_gene (*120210) on chromosome 6p13. One<br>single family has been reported.|A 30-year-old proband was reported with knee pains and difficulty walking since<br>10 years of age. Radiographs showed early osteoarthritis of one knee, Schmorl<br>nodes, endplate irregularities, anterior osteophytes in the thoracolumbar<br>vertebrae and normal hips. The mother had the same variant but she did not<br>reveal any symptom before age 45 years.|\n|Schmid-type<br>metaphyseal<br>chondrodysplasia<br>(MCDS)|**COL10**|MCDS (#156500) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL10A1_(*120110) gene on chromosome 6q22. MCDS<br>is transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait.|MCDS is a rare genetic disorder characterized by short stature, short arms and legs<br>(short-limbed dwarfism) and bowing of the long bones. Radiographic features<br>include widening and irregularity of the growth plates, especially in the distal and<br>proximal femora. These defects give rise to unusual “waddling” walk (gait).|\n|Marshall syndrome<br>(MRSHS)|**COL11**|MRSHS (#154780) is an autosomal dominant genetic<br>disorder caused by variants in_COL11A1_gene (*120280)<br>on chromosome 1p21.|Patients have a distinctive flat midface with a flattened nasal bridge (saddle nose),<br>nostrils that turn upward, widely spaced eyes, high myopia, cataracts, and<br>sensorineural hearing loss. Other symptoms include crossed eyes (esotropia),<br>retinal detachment, glaucoma, protruding upper incisors (teeth), and a small or<br>missing nasal bone.|\n|Fibrochondrogenesis-1<br>(FBCG1)|**COL11**|FBCG1 (#228520) is a severe, autosomal recessive disorder<br>caused by variants in_COL11A1_gene (*120280) on<br>chromosome 1p21.|FBCG1 and FBCG2 are short-limbed skeletal dysplasia frequently lethal. The disorder<br>is named for the disorganized cartilage growth plate in which chondrocytes have a<br>fibroblastic appearance and the presence of fibrous cartilage extracellular matrix.<br>i|\n|Fibrochondrogenesis-2<br>(FBCG2)||FBCG2 (#614524) can have an autosomal recessive or<br>dominant inheritance due to variants in_COL11A2_gene<br>(*120290) on chromosome 6p21.3.|Patients are characterized by short stature (dwarfism) and skeletal abnormalities.<br>Affected individuals have shortened long bones in the arms and legs that are<br>unusually wide at the ends (described as dumbbell-shaped). Hands and feet are<br>relatively normal. Vertebrae are flattened (platyspondyly) and have a characteristic<br>pinched or pear shape that is noticeable on X-rays. Ribs are typically short and<br>wide and have metaphyseal cupping at both ends. Affected infants have a very<br>narrow chest, which prevents the lungs from developing normally. Most infants are<br>stillborn or die shortly after birth from respiratory failure. Some affected individuals<br>have lived into childhood. Affected individuals who survive the neonatal period have<br>high myopia, mild to moderate hearing loss and severe skeletal dysplasia.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.187 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Autosomal dominant<br>deafness-13<br>(DFNA13)|**COL11**|DFNA13 (#601868) is an autosomal dominant disorder<br>caused by heterozygous variant in_COL11A2_gene<br>(*120290) on chromosome 6p21.|A single family has been described, characterized by a dominant nonsyndromic<br>postlingual hearing loss. The affected individuals experienced progressive<br>hearing loss beginning in the second to fourth decades.|\n|Otospondylo-<br>megaepiphyseal<br>dysplasia,<br>autosomal<br>dominant<br>(OSMEDA)|**COL11**|The autosomal dominant OSMEDA (#184840), also known<br>as Weissenbacher-Zweymuller syndrome (WZS), is<br>caused by heterozygous variant in_COL11A2_gene<br>(*120290) on chromosome 6p21. The disorder has an<br>autosomal dominant transmission.|OSMED is characterized by skeletal abnormalities, distinctive facial features and<br>severe hearing loss. The term “otospondylomegaepiphyseal” refers to the parts<br>of the body that are affected: ears (oto-), bones of the spine (spondylo-) and the<br>ends (epiphyses) of long bones in the arms and legs. The disorder is<br>characterized by sensorineural hearing loss, relatively short extremities with<br>abnormally large knees and elbows (enlarged epiphyses), vertebral body<br>i|\n|Autosomal recessive<br>(OSMEDB)||The autosomal recessive OSMEDB (#215150) is also<br>caused by variant in the_COL11A2_gene.|anomalies and characteristic facies. The diagnostic radiologic findings are<br>enlarged epiphyses combined with moderate platyspondyly, mainly in the lower<br>thoracic region. No ocular abnormalities are reported. Patients have typical facial<br>features, including midface hypoplasia.|\n|Congenital<br>myasthenic<br>syndrome type 19<br>(CMS19)|**COL13**|CMS19 (#616720) is an autosomal recessive disorder<br>resulting from variants in_COL13A1_gene (*120350) on<br>chromosome 10q22.|The congenital myasthenic syndromes (CMSs) are a heterogeneous group of<br>inherited disorders resulting from impaired neuromuscular transmission and<br>caused by variants in genes involved in the formation or integrity of<br>neuromuscular junctions (NMJs). CMS19 result in generalized muscle weakness,<br>exercise intolerance and respiratory insufficiency. Patients present hypotonia,<br>feeding difficulties and respiratory problems soon after birth. The severity of the<br>weakness and disease course is variable.|\n|Epithelial recurrent<br>erosion dystrophy<br>(ERED)|**COL17**|ERED (#122400) is caused by heterozygous variant in<br>_COL17A1_gene (*113811) on chromosome 10q24. The<br>disorder is transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait.|ERED is characterized by bilaterally painful recurrent corneal erosions. Erosions<br>often are precipitated by relatively minor trauma and are often difficult to treat,<br>lasting for up to a week. Fortunately, the erosions become less frequent as<br>patients age and may cease altogether by the fifth decade of life. The onset is in<br>the first decade of life (even in the first year of life) often with some subepithelial<br>haze or blebs while denser centrally located opacities develop with time. Small<br>grey anterior stromal flecks associated with larger focal gray-white disc-shaped,<br>circular or wreath-like lesions with central clarity, in the Bowman layer and<br>immediately subjacent anterior stroma, varying from 0.2 to 1.5 mm in diameter,<br>may be diagnostic of ERED.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.188 -->\n\n### **TABLE 10.1 Hereditary Disorders Resulting From Collagen Structural Alterations—cont’d** \n\n|Disorder|Collagen<br>Type|<br>Genetic Alteration<sup>†</sup>|Major Clinical Features<sup>†</sup>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Knobloch<br>syndrome-1<br>(KNO1)|**COL18**|KNO1 (#267750) is a hereditary autosomal recessive<br>disorder caused by variants in_COL18A1_gene (*120328)<br>on chromosome 21q22.3.|KNO1 is primarily characterized by severe vision problems and skull defects. Eye<br>abnormalities include high myopia, cataracts, dislocated lens, vitreoretinal<br>degeneration and retinal detachment. Skull defects range from occipital<br>encephalocele to occult cutis aplasia.|\n|Congenital fibrosis of<br>extraocular<br>muscles-5<br>(CFEOM5)|**COL25**|CFEOM5 (#616219) has an autosomal recessive<br>inheritance and is caused by variants in_COL25A1_gene<br>(*610004) on chromosome 4q25. A single family had<br>been reported so far.|CFEOM include several different inherited strabismus syndromes characterized by<br>congenital restrictive ophthalmoplegia affecting extraocular muscles innervated<br>by the oculomotor and/or trochlear nerves. CFEOM5 has been reported in a<br>single family with 3 sibs showing a congenital cranial dysinnervation affecting the<br>ocular muscles. The patients had variable abnormal ocular motility without other<br>systemic defects. Two sibs showed congenital ptosis with levator palpebrae<br>muscle dysinnervation of one or both orbits. The levator palpebrae muscle was<br>normally innervated by cranial nerve III (oculomotor nerve). The third sib had no<br>ptosis but showed bilateral Duane retraction syndrome, exotropic in the right<br>eye and esotropic in the left.|\n|Steel syndrome<br>(STLS)<br>*Only hereditary disorders r<br>†Information mainly based<br>_COL,_Collagen. The acrony      i<br>Modified from Arseni L, Lo|**COL27**<br>esulting in stru<br>on OMIM, the<br>ms of the path   i<br>i    mbardi A, Orio|STLS (#615155) displays an autosomal recessive<br>inheritance due to variants in_COL27A1_gene (*608461)<br>on 9q32 chromosome. Few cases have been reported<br>who belong to the same family.<br>ctural collagen alterations are listed.<br>Online Mendelian Index in Man athttp://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query<br>ologies are all specified inside the table.<br>i      lo: From structure to phenotype: impact of collagen alterations on human h|Patients affected by STLS present a characteristic facies, dislocated hips and<br>radial heads, carpal coalition (fusion of carpal bones), short stature, scoliosis,<br>and cervical spine anomalies. The dislocated hips are resistant to surgical<br>intervention.<br>.fcgi?db=OMIM. Collagen types are in bold.<br>i                 ealth._Int J Mol Sci_. 2018;19:1407 (Table 1).|\n\n<!-- p.189 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.2** Hereditary i Disorders Associated to Reduced Syn i thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific i Collagen Types\n\n|Disorder|Genetic Alteration|Link with the Disorder|Major Clinical Features*|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Autosomal recessive<br>dystrophic<br>epidermolysis<br>bullosa (RDEB)|A defect in collagenase MMP1<br>(*120353) has been implicated in<br>RDEB (#226600). An association<br>between disease severity and specific<br>SNP in MMP1 gene (*120353) was<br>found in three affected members of<br>one family and in a cohort of 31<br>unrelated French RDEB patients. The<br>SNP results in increased transcript<br>and active MMP1 protein levels.|COL7 is susceptible to degradation by the<br>collagenase matrix metalloproteinases-1<br>(MMP1). An imbalance between COL7<br>synthesis and degradation could conceivably<br>worsen the RDEB phenotype.|Patients with RDEB present generalized blisters at birth that<br>result in extensive scarring and pseudosyndactyly. After<br>birth, extensive blisters may affect the mucous<br>membranes particularly the oral cavity, esophagus and<br>anal canal. Caused by chronic blood loss, inflammation,<br>infection and poor nutrition, patients develop anemia,<br>failure to thrive, delayed puberty and osteoporosis.<br>Patients usually do not survive more than 30 years due to<br>severe renal complications or aggressive squamous cell<br>carcinoma arising in the areas of repeated scarring.|\n|Aneurysm, abdominal<br>aortic (AAA)|<br>Mapped loci for AAA (#100070) include<br>AAA1 (*100070) on chromosome<br>19q13, AAA2 (*609782) on<br>chromosome 4q31, AAA3 (*611891)<br>on chromosome 9p21 and AAA4<br>(*614375) on chromosome 12q13.<br>Inheritance is autosomal dominant.|Several studies pointed to a role of MMPs in the<br>end-stage of AAA. MMPs are enzymes capable<br>of degrading connective tissue that may affect<br>arterial walls by degrading collagens and other<br>ECM components. Polymorphisms in MMP2,<br>MMP3, MMP9, and MMP13 genes result in<br>increased protein levels significantly associated<br>to AAA risk.|AAA is characterized by chronic inflammation and ECM<br>degradation of the aortic wall.<br>The main symptoms of this condition are dysphasia,<br>frontotemporal cerebral atrophy and frontotemporal<br>dementia, speech disorder, memory impairment.|\n|Trichothiodystrophy<br>1, photosensitive<br>form (TTD1)|TTD1 (#601675) is caused by<br>homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variant in the ERCC2/<br>XPD gene (*126340) on chromosome<br>19q13. The gene encodes a helicase<br>subunit of the transcription/repair<br>factor TFIIH. The inheritance is<br>autosomal recessive.|A reduced expression of COL6A1 (*120220), an<br>abundant collagen of skin and connective<br>tissue, has been shown in the skin of TTD<br>patients with variants in the ERCC2/XPD gene.<br>It has been shown that specific transcription<br>deregulations in the cells of TTD patients with<br>variants in the ERCC2/XPD gene result in the<br>overexpression of MMP1 gene. This event leads<br>to hypersecretion of active MMP1 enzyme and<br>degradation of collagen type I in the dermis of<br>TTD patient skin.|<br>TTD is characterized by hair abnormalities, physical and<br>mental retardation, ichthyosis, signs of premature aging,<br>and cutaneous photosensitivity. The clinical spectrum of<br>TTD varies widely from patients with only brittle, fragile<br>hair to patients with the most severe neuroectodermal<br>symptoms. TTD patients present sulfur-deficient brittle<br>hair with a diagnostic alternating light and dark banding<br>pattern (called “tiger” tail banding) under polarizing<br>microscopy. Common additional clinical features include<br>collodion baby, characteristic facies, ocular abnormalities,<br>short stature, decreased fertility, and recurrent infections.<br>TTD patients present a 20-fold higher mortality compared<br>to the US general population.|\n|Atopic dermatitis<br>(ATOD)|ATOD (#603165) is caused by the<br>presence of a specific SNP<br>(rs4688761) in COL29A1 gene<br>(*611916), which encodes a novel<br>epidermal collagen. The gene is on<br>chromosome 3q22.1.<br>Inheritance is autosomal dominant.|COL29A1 shows a specific gene expression<br>pattern with the highest transcript levels in skin,<br>lung and gastrointestinal tract, which are the<br>major sites of allergic disease manifestation.<br>Lack of COL29A1 expression in the outer layers<br>of the epidermis of ATOD patients points to a<br>role of collagen XXIX in epidermal integrity,<br>whose breakdown is a clinical hallmark of AD.|<br>ATOD is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized<br>by intensely itchy skin lesions.<br>The onset of disease is typically observed during the first 2<br>years of life. The hallmarks of atopic dermatitis are a<br>chronic relapsing form of skin inflammation, a disturbance<br>of epidermal barrier function that culminates in dry skin<br>and IgE-mediated sensitization to food and environmental<br>allergens.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.190 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.2** Hereditary i Disorders Associated to Reduced Syn i thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific C i ollagen Types—cont’d\n\n|Disorder|Genetic Alteration|Link with the Disorder|Major Clinical Features*|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Bruck syndrome<br>(BRKS)|BRKS is a very rare autosomal recessive<br>syndrome. Two forms are found:<br>BRKS1 (#259450) is caused by<br>variants in FKBP10 (*607067) gene<br>whereas BRKS2 (#609220) by<br>variants in PLOD2 (*601865) gene.||BRKS is characterized by bone fragility associated with<br>congenital joint contractures. Patients commonly show<br>short stature, skull wormian bones, and kyphoscoliosis.<br>Most cases had normal teeth, white sclera, normal<br>cognitive functions and normal hearing. A few cases had<br>dysmorphic features including triangular face and<br>brachycephaly.|\n|Bruck syndrome 1<br>(BRKS1)|BRKS1 (#259450) is caused by<br>homozygous variants in FKBP10<br>gene (*607063) on chromosome<br>17q21 resulting in FKBP65 loss of<br>function.<br>Inheritance is autosomal recessive.|Variants in FKBP10 result in delay of type 1<br>procollagen secretion, incomplete stabilization<br>of collagen trimer, reduced hydroxylation of the<br>telopeptide lysyl residue (involved in<br>intermolecular collagen cross-linking).|BRKS1 patients have short stature, high incidence of joint<br>contractures, frequent fractures and scoliosis.|\n|Bruck syndrome 2<br>(BRKS2)|BRKS2 (#609220) is caused by<br>homozygous variant in PLOD2 gene<br>(*601865) on chromosome 3q24.<br>Inheritance is autosomal recessive.|PLOD2 encodes the telopeptide lysyl hydroxylase<br>required for the triple-helical crosslinking of<br>collagen molecules. Variants in this gene affect<br>the instalment and secretion of collagen fibers<br>from osteoblasts.|No phenotypic differences between BRKS1 and BRKS2<br>have been reported.|\n|Ehlers-Danlos<br>syndrome (EDS)<br>subtypes|The EDS subtypes are due to variants in<br>several genes, including PLOD1,<br>FKBP14, ADAMTS2, ZNF469, and<br>PRDM5.|||\n|EDS Kyphoscoliotic<br>Type 1<br>(EDSKSCL1)|EDSKSCL1 (#225400) previously<br>designated EDS6, is caused by<br>homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variant in the<br>PLOD1 (*153454) gene on<br>chromosome 1p36.<br>Inheritance is autosomal recessive.|PLOD1 encodes a lysyl hydroxylase that catalyzes<br>the hydroxylation of lysine residues in X-lys-gly<br>sequences of collagens and other proteins with<br>collagen-like domains.<br>This hydroxylation is essential for the stability of<br>intermolecular collagen crosslinks.|EDSKSCL1 is characterized by skin fragility (easy bruising,<br>friable skin, poor wound healing, widened atrophic<br>scarring), scleral and ocular fragility/rupture, microcornea,<br>facial dysmorphology. General features also include<br>congenital muscle hypotonia, congenital or early-onset<br>kyphoscoliosis, joint hypermobility with subluxations or<br>dislocations of shoulders, hips, and knees.|\n|EDS Kyphoscoliotic<br>Type, 2<br>(EDSKSCL2)|EDSKSCL2 (#614557) is caused by<br>homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variants in<br>FKBP14 gene (*614505) on<br>chromosome 7p15.<br>Inheritance is autosomal recessive.|FKBP14 is an ER-resident protein belonging to the<br>family of FK506-binding peptidyl-prolyl cis–trans<br>isomerases (PPIases). It catalyses the folding of<br>COL3 and interacts with COL3, COL4, and<br>COLX.|EDSKSCL2 is characterized by congenital hearing<br>impairment (sensorineural, conductive, or mixed), follicular<br>hyperkeratosis, muscle atrophy, bladder diverticula.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.191 -->\n\n|**Hereditary D         i**<br>**TABLE**<br>**10.2**|<br>**isorders Associated to Reduced Sy     i**|<br>**nthesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific**|<br>**i Collagen Types—cont’d**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Disorder|Genetic Alteration|Link with the Disorder|Major Clinical Features*|\n|EDS dermatosparaxis<br>Type (EDSDERMS)|EDSDERMS (#225410) is caused by<br>variant in ADAMTS2 (*604539) gene<br>on chromosome 5q35.<br>Inheritance is autosomal recessive.|ADAMTS2 encodes a procollagen protease that<br>takes part to the processing of type I<br>procollagen.|Dermatosparaxis means “tearing of skin.” Patients present<br>extreme skin laxity and fragility, easy bruising, extensive<br>scar formation, and joint laxity. Blue sclerae,<br>micrognathia, umbilical hernia and postnatal growth<br>faltering are reported.|\n|Brittle Cornea<br>Syndrome1 (BCS1)|BCS1 (#229200) can be caused by<br>homozygous variant in the ZNF469<br>gene (*612078) on chromosome<br>16q24. Inheritance is autosomal<br>recessive.|ZNF469 encodes a zinc-finger protein that likely<br>acts as a transcription factor or extra-nuclear<br>regulator factor for the synthesis or organization<br>of collagen fibers.|BCS1 and BCS2 are associated with retinal microvascular<br>abnormalities, keratoconus or keratoglobus, blue sclerae,<br>extreme corneal thinning and a high risk of corneal<br>rupture. Hyperelasticity of the skin without excessive<br>fragility and hypermobility of the joints are other hallmarks<br>of the disease.|\n|Brittle Cornea<br>Syndrome2 (BCS2)|BCS2 (#614170) is caused by variant in<br>PRDM5 gene (*614161) on<br>chromosome 4q27. Inheritance is<br>autosomal recessive.|PRDM5 seems to regulate the expression of<br>proteins involved in extracellular matrix<br>development and maintenance, including<br>COL4A1 and COL11A1.|BCS2 features overlap with BCS1. Systemic abnormalities<br>included increased skin laxity, pectus excavatum,<br>scoliosis, congenital hip dislocation, recurrent shoulder<br>dislocation, high-frequency hearing loss, high-arched<br>palate, and mitral valve prolapse.|\n|CUTIS LAXA|Cutis laxa can be caused by variants in<br>either PYCR1 (*179035) or<br>ALDH18A1 (#614438) gene.||Cutis laxa is a rare skin disorder characterized by wrinkled,<br>redundant, inelastic and sagging skin due to defective<br>synthesis of elastic fibers and other proteins of the ECM.|\n|Cutis Laxa,<br>autosomal<br>recessive Type IIB<br>(ARCL2B)|ARCL2B (#612940) is caused by<br>homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variant in the PYCR1<br>gene (*179035) on chromosome<br>17q25.3. Inheritance is autosomal<br>recessive.|PYCR1 encodes the enzyme pyrroline-5-<br>carboxylate reductase1, which catalyzes the<br>last step of proline synthesis. PYCR1 deficiency<br>can affect the proper collagen formation.|ARCL2 is a more benign form of cutis laxa present at birth.<br>Growth and developmental delay and skeletal anomalies<br>are reported. Intellectual deficit and seizures have been<br>reported in older patients. Systemic manifestations are<br>mild whereas pulmonary emphysema and cardiac<br>anomalies are rare.|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.192 -->\n\n|**Hereditary D         i**<br>**TABLE**<br>**10.2**|<br>**isorders Associated to Reduced Syn     i**|<br>**thesis or Excessive Degradation of Specific**|<br>**i Collagen Types—cont’d**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Disorder|Genetic Alteration|Link with the Disorder|Major Clinical Features*|\n|Cutis Laxa,<br>autosomal<br>recessive Type IIIB<br>(ARCL3B)|ARCL3B (#614438) is caused by variant<br>in PYCR1 gene (179035) on<br>chromosome 17q25. Inheritance is<br>autosomal recessive.||ARCL3B is a rare autosomal recessive disorder<br>characterized by a progeria-like appearance with<br>distinctive facial features, sparse hair, ophthalmologic<br>abnormalities, and intrauterine growth retardation.|\n|Cutis Laxa,<br>autosomal<br>recessive, Type IIIA<br>(ARCL3A)|ARCL3A (#219150) is caused by variant<br>in the ALDH18A1 gene (*138250) on<br>chromosome 10q24. Inheritance is<br>autosomal recessive.|The protein encoded by ALDH18A1 catalyzes the<br>reduction of glutamate to delta1-pyrroline-5-<br>carboxylate, a critical step in the de novo<br>biosynthesis of proline, ornithine and arginine.|ARCL3A is characterized by cutis laxa (a progeria-like<br>appearance) and ophthalmologic abnormalities. In some<br>cases, additional features have been described, including<br>delayed development, intellectual disability, seizures, and<br>problems with movement that can worsen over time.|\n|Cutis Laxa,<br>autosomal<br>dominant, Type III<br>(ADCL3)|ADCL3 (#616603) is caused by variant<br>in ALDH18A1 gene (*138250) on<br>chromosome 10q24. Inheritance is<br>autosomal dominant.||ADCL3 has a progeroid appearance characterized by thin<br>skin with visible veins and wrinkles, ophthalmological<br>abnormalities, clenched fingers, pre-and postnatal<br>growth retardation, and moderate intellectual disability.<br>Patients also exhibit a combination of muscular hypotonia<br>with brisk muscle reflexes..|\n|Keratoconus-1<br>(KTCN1)<br>*Information mainly based o<br>The acronyms of the patho   i<br>From Arseni L, Lombardi A|KTCN1 (#148300) is caused by<br>heterozygous variant in the Visual<br>system homeobox gene 1 (VSX1,<br>*605020) gene on 20p11<br>chromosome. Inheritance is<br>autosomal dominant.<br>n OMIM, the Online Mendelian Index in Man athttp:<br>logies are all specified inside the table.<br>, Oriolo D. From structure to phenotype: impact of c|VSX1 encodes a homeoprotein that regulates the<br>expression of the cone opsin genes early in<br>development.<br>Recent studies showed that the structural<br>deformity of the cornea in KCTN patients may<br>be due to reduced expression of collagens<br>(COL1A1 and COL4A1) and LOX family<br>oxidases, as well as on the concomitant<br>increased expression of MMP9.<br>//www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?db=OMIM.<br>ollagen alterations on human health._Int J Mol Sci_. 2018;19:1407|KTCN1 is the most common corneal dystrophy. It is a<br>bilateral, often asymmetrical, noninflammatory progressive<br>corneal ectasia that causes visual morbidity. In affected<br>individuals, the cornea becomes progressively thin and<br>conical in shape, resulting in myopia, irregular<br>astigmatism, and corneal scarring. It typically appears in<br>the teenage years and then it progresses until the third<br>and fourth decades. No specific treatment exists except<br>corneal transplantation when visual acuity can no longer<br>be corrected by contact lenses.<br>(Table 2).|\n\n<!-- p.193 -->\n\n|**TABLE**<br><br>**No**<br>|**n-exhausti**<br>|**ve List of Genetic Diseases Cause**<br>|**d by Defects in Core Connective Tissue Components and a**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**10.3**<br>**Su**|**mmary of**|**elated Phenotypes**||\n|Protein|Gene(s)|Disease|Key Manifestations|\n|Aggrecan|_ACAN_|Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia,<br>Kimberley type|Short stature, progressive osteoarthropathy, spondyloepiphyseal<br>dysplasia|\n|||Short stature and advanced bone<br>age, with or without early-onset<br>osteoarthritis and/or<br>osteochondritis dissecans|Short stature, advanced bone age, with or without early-onset<br>osteoarthritis and/or osteochondritis dissecans|\n|||Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia,<br>aggrecan type|Severe short stature, spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia|\n|Biglycan|_BGN_|Meester-Loeys syndrome|Aortic aneurysm or dissection, orofacial features, joint<br>hypermobility; ventricular dilatation on brain imaging, relative<br>macrocephaly; hip dislocation; platyspondyly, phalangeal<br>dysplasia, dysplastic epiphyses of the long bones|\n|||X-linked sponyloepimetphyseal<br>dysplasia|Severe short-trunked dwarfism, brachydactyly,<br>spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia|\n|||Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia|Mildly short stature, early-onset osteoarthritis|\n|Decorin|_DCN_|Congenital stromal corneal dystrophy|Corneal stromal opacification, visual loss, increased corneal<br>thickness|\n|Elastin|_ELN_|Cutis laxa|Wrinkled, redundant, sagging inelastic skin|\n|||Williams syndrome|Cardiovascular disease (esp. supravalvular aortic stenosis),<br>orofacial features; intellectual deficit, connective tissue<br>abnormalities; endocrine abnormalities|\n|Fibrillin-1|_FBN1_|Marfan syndrome|Aortic root aneurysm or dissection, ectopia lentis, marfanoid<br>habitus|\n|||Weill-Marchesani-syndrome|Short stature, brachydactyly, joint stiffness and contractures, toe<br>walking, muscular build, early-onset carpal tunnel syndrome,<br>ectopia lentis, microspherophakia, thick skin, maxillary<br>hypoplasia|\n|||Stiff skin syndrome|Relative short stature, brachydactyly, joint stiffness and<br>contractures, diffuse entrapment neuropathy including carpal<br>tunnel syndrome, progressive thick, hard skin, cutaneous<br>nodules|\n|||Geleophysic dysplasia|Short stature, brachydactyly, joint stiffness and contractures, toe<br>walking, early onset carpal tunnel syndrome, progressive<br>thickening of cardiac valves, thick skin, tracheal stenosis,<br>respiratory insufficiency, full cheeks, a short nose,<br>hypertelorism, a thin upper lip (“happy face”)|\n|||Acromelic dysplasia|Short stature, brachydactyly, joint stiffness and contractures,<br>pseudomuscular build, notch femoral head, early onset carpal<br>tunnel syndrome, thick skin, round face, a bulbous nose, thick<br>lips, short palpebral fissures, hoarse voice|\n|Fibrillin-2|_FBN2_|Congenital contractural<br>arachnodactyly (CCA) or<br>Beals-Hecht Syndrome|Tall stature, arachnodactyly, (kypho)scoliosis, pectus deformities,<br>contractures, muscle hypoplasia, mild cardiovascular<br>involvement, long, narrow face, highly arched palate,<br>micrognathia, crumpled external ears|\n|||Acromelic dysplasia|Relative short stature, brachydactyly, toe walking, early onset<br>carpal tunnel syndrome, short palpebral fissures|\n|Fibronectin|_FN_|Glomerulopathy with fibronectin<br>deposits 2|Glomerulopathy with fibronectin deposits|\n|||Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia,<br>corner fracture type|Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia characterized by flake-like,<br>triangular, or curvilinear ossification centers at the edges of<br>irregular metaphyses that simulate fractures, short stature|\n|Fibulin 4|_FBLN4_|Cutis laxa|Arterial tortuosity, aneurysms and stenoses, mild cutis laxa|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.194 -->\n\n|**TABLE**|<br>**Non-exhaustive List of Genetic Diseases Caused by Defects in Core Connective Tissue Components and a**|\n|---|---|\n|**10.3**|**Summary of Related Phenotypes—cont’d**|\n\n\n|Protein|Gene(s)|Disease|Key Manifestations|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Fibulin 5|_FBLN5_|Cutis laxa|Severe cutis laxa, supravalvular aortic stenosis, peripheral<br>pulmonary artery stenosis, severe pulmonary emphysema,<br>bladder diverticulae|\n|LTBP2|_LTBP2_||Ectopia lentis|\n|LTPB3|_LTBP3_|Geleophysical dysplasia|Short stature, short hands and feet, joint contractures, distinctive<br>facial features, progressive cardiac valvular disease, and<br>thickened skin|\n|||Dental Anomalies and Short Stature|Disproportionate short stature, amelogenesis imperfecta|\n|||Acromicric dysplasia|Short stature, pseudomuscular build, stiff joints, tight skin|\n|||Thoracic aortic aneurysm and<br>dissection|Thoracic and abdominal aortic aneurysm and dissections, short<br>stature, dental anomalies|\n|LTBP4<br>_LTBP,_Latent|_LTBP4_<br>TGFβ-binding p|Cutis laxa<br>roteins.|Severe cutis laxa, supravalvular aortic stenosis, peripheral<br>pulmonary artery stenosis, severe pulmonary emphysema,<br>bladder diverticula, gastrointestinal complications (diverticula,<br>elongation and tortuosity of GI tract, rectal prolapse,<br>diaphragmatic involvement)|\n\n\norganized fibrils. The fibrils are arranged in a staggered fashion that produces a characteristic 67 nm repeating banding pattern referred to as the _D-period_ that is visible under electron microscopy. Type I collagen, a heterotrimer composed of two α1 chains and one α2 chain, is the most abundant fibrillar collagen; type II and type III collagens are also abundant, and together the 3 are referred to as _major fibrillar collagens_ . The other fibril-forming collagens, types V, XI, XXIV, and XXVII, are less abundant and are known as _minor fibrillar collagens_ . \n\nType IX, XII, XIV, XVI, XIX, XX, XXI, and XXII collagens are referred to as **fibril-associated collagens with interrupted triple helices (FACITs)** . These structurally diverse collagens contain short triple helical domains intersected by several non-collagenous domains. FACITs do not form fibrils themselves but rather associate with the surface of fibrillar collagens. Type IX collagen associates with type II collagen fibrils in cartilage, whereas type XII and XIV collagens are found in the vicinity of type I collagen fibrils in skin and tendon, respectively. This tissue-specific expression molds the surface of fibrillar collagens and confers certain tissue-specific biomechanical properties. \n\n**Network-forming collagens** (types IV, VI, VIII, and X) associate in multiple dimensions and form open networks rather than fibers. Type IV collagen is the major collagen of all basement membranes, forming an elaborate supramolecular scaffold or polygonal lattice often described as a chickenwire network. Type VI collagen, a major component of muscle ECM that contributes to the basement membrane of myofiber endomysium, undergoes a complex multistep process of intracellular assembly of monomers into dimers and tetramers. Following secretion, the tetramers associate \n\nend-to-end into beaded microfilaments. Type VIII and type X collagens form hexagonal networks in the Descemet membrane of the eye and in hypertrophic cartilage, respectively, whereas type VI collagen assembles into beaded filaments. \n\n## **Collagen Biosynthesis** \n\nThe transcriptional regulation of collagen biosynthesis is largely dependent on the cell type but is also under the control of growth factors and cytokines, such as transforming growth factor β (TGFβ), insulin-like growth factor, and fibroblast growth factor, as well as their respective promotor binding sites (Fig. 10.1). After transcription, translation, and removal of the signal peptide, the pro-α chains (i.e., the α chains with N- and C- pro-peptides still attached) undergo extensive post-translational modification in the endoplasmic reticulum prior to and during folding. _4-hydroxylation of proline residues_ at the Y position of a Gly-Xaa-Yaa repeat is catalyzed by a tetramer of protein disulfide isomerase (PDI) and prolyl 4-hydroxylase (P4H). This post-translational modification is common: a quarter of the amino acids in mammalian fibrillar α chains are prolyl residues, and approximately 50% of these residues are 4-hydroxylated. The resulting 4-hydroxyprolines confer stability to the folded triple helical structure through hydrogen bonding and stereoelectronic effects; collagen molecules lacking 4-hydroxyproline residues are unable to form triple helices that are stable at physiologic temperatures. \n\nWhile 4-hydroxylation is a frequent post-translational modification, _3-hydroxylation of proline residues_ is less common and is carried out by an enzyme complex composed of prolyl 3-hydroxylase 1 (P3H1 or Lepre1), cartilage-associated\n\n<!-- p.195 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0196_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.1** Biosynthesis of a “prototype” collagen. The procollagen α-chains are synthesized by ribosomes in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Already during the synthesis of the nascent polypeptide, certain prolyl and lysyl residues are hydroxylated and modified by glycosylation. Three α-chains associate to form a trimer and fold into a triple helix. This process is facilitated by heat shock protein (HSP) 47, a collagen-specific molecular chaperone in the ER. The transport and Golgi organization (TANGO) protein 1 mediates secretion of the newly formed triple helical procollagen into the extracellular space, where the N- and C-terminal propeptides are cleaved by specific proteases. The mature collagen molecules assemble to form mixed fibrils with other collagens and non-collagenous molecules. The suprastructures are stabilized by covalent crosslinks. Small leucine-rich proteoglycans (SLRPs) and other proteoglycans have roles in the regulation of collagen fibrillogenesis and crosslinking. _EDS,_ Ehlers–Danlos syndrome. (From Nyström A, Bruckner-Tuderman L: Biology of the extracellular matrix in Bolognia JL, Schaffer JV, Cerroni [editors] Dermatology 5/e, Vol 2, 2025, Elsevier, Philadelphia [Fig. 95.2, p. 1695]. Courtesy of the Yale Dermatology Residents’ Slide Collection.)\n\n<!-- p.196 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0197_01.jpg)\n\n\nprotein (CRTAP or Leprel3), and cyclophilin B (CypB). The exact role of 3-hydroxyproline residues is unknown, though their presence confers specific biomechanical properties and influences collagen fiber assembly; a severe autosomal recessive form of osteogenesis imperfecta is related to pathogenic variants in _P3H1_ . \n\n_Hydroxylation of lysine residues_ is necessary for the creation of crosslinks that confer tensile strength and mechanical stability to collagen. Three distinct lysyl hydroxylases catalyze the hydroxylation of lysine residues in collagen telopeptides and in the helical domain. Some triple helical domain hydroxylysines are subsequently O-glycosylated in the Golgi apparatus. Telopeptide lysine and hydroxylysine residues serve as substrates for lysyl oxidase, and the resulting aldehydes form crosslinks with helical lysines, hydroxylysines, and glycosylated sidechains, providing tensile strength and mechanical stability to collagen. \n\nIn addition to its role in 4-hydroxylation of proline residues, protein disulfide isomerase catalyzes the formation of intrachain and interchain disulfide bonds in the C-terminal pro-peptides, which initiates the assembly of three pro-α chains into a superhelix. The subsequent folding propagates from the C- to the N-terminus in a zipper-like fashion and is assisted by endoplasmic reticulum chaperones such as calnexin, calreticulin, binding immunoglobulin protein, GRP94, and PDI, as well as procollagen-specific molecular chaperones such as heat shock protein 47 (Hsp47) and FKBP65. \n\nThe modified and folded procollagen molecules are transported from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi apparatus. A complex consisting of the transmembrane protein TANGO1 (transport and Golgi organization 1) and Hsp47 directs collagen molecules into special vesicles at endoplasmic reticulum exit sites; however, the additional steps required to package these large procollagen molecules (up to 400 nm in length) into small secretory vesicles (ranging in size from 60 nm to 90 nm) are poorly understood. Procollagens later aggregate in the Golgi apparatus to form intermediate fibrils, which are transported to the extracellular space within secretory vacuoles known as Golgi-to-plasma membrane carriers. \n\nThe processing of the procollagen pro-peptides is a critical step prior to fibrillogenesis and is catalyzed by several enzymes. In major fibrillar collagens, members of the ADAMTS (a disintegrin and metalloproteinase with thrombospondin type 1 motifs) family (i.e., ADAMTS-2, ADAMTS-3, and ADAMTS-14) catalyze N-pro-peptide cleavage. The removal of the C-pro-peptide is performed by members of the bone morphogenetic protein-1/tolloid-like proteinase family. Alternatively, the metalloproteinases meprin α and meprin β facilitate the processing of both the N- and C- pro-peptides in type I and type III collagens. Removal of the pro-peptides in minor fibrillar collagens differs from that of the major fibrillar collagens. The release of the pro-peptides decreases the solubility of the resulting collagen about 1,000-fold, and the entropic energy that is released drives the self-assembly of the collagen into fibrils. Self-assembled collagen fibers have considerable tensile \n\nstrength, but their strength is increased further by crosslinking reactions that form covalent bonds between α chains in one molecule and α chains in adjacent molecules. The resulting fibers, composed of hundreds or thousands of triplehelical monomers, have some of the properties of a crystal but have innate imperfections that make them highly flexible. \n\nThe intermediate collagen fibrils assemble into striated fibrils through linear and lateral fusion. Although the assembly of collagen monomers into fibers is largely a spontaneous reaction, this complex process requires the concerted action of several assisting proteins. The site of fibril assembly is determined in part by _organizers_ such as fibronectin and integrins. _Nucleators_ such as collagens type V and XI initiate collagen fibrillogenesis, and _regulators_ such as small leucine-rich proteoglycans and tenascin-X modulate the rate of assembly and the size and structure of collagen fibrils. The specific composition and function of these assisting proteins determine the ultimate ultrastructural characteristics of the assembled fibers, such as being assembled into large tendons in some tissues, while in others, into sheets, and in still others into complex structures such as the hexagonal array of fibers that provide both the strength and transparency of the cornea. \n\nCollagen fibers are resistant to most proteases, but during degradation of connective tissues, they are cleaved by specific **matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs)** termed **collagenases** that cause partial unfolding of the triple helices into gelatin-like structures that are further degraded by less specific proteinases. \n\n# **Microfibrils and Elastic Fibers** \n\nIn addition to tensile strength, many tissues such as the lung, large blood vessels, skin, and ligaments require elasticity. This tissue elasticity is provided by fibrillin-rich **microfibrils** and **elastic fibers** , insoluble ECM assemblies that permit long-range deformability and recoil. Microfibrils are filamentous macromolecules with tissue-specific compositions that provide mechanical stability, structural integrity, and elasticity to tissues. Microfibrils also sequester **multiple growth factors** including TGFβ and bone morphogenic protein either via direct binding or through interaction with latent TGFβ-binding proteins. Isolated un-tensioned microfibrils from mammalian tissues such as skin and ligament have a repeating “beads-on-a-string” appearance with an average though variable periodicity of 56 nm. In tissues, microfibrils are generally organized into loose, roughly parallel bundles, the principal component of which is **fibrillin** . The three members of the fibrillin protein family (fibrillin-1, fibrillin-2, and fibrillin-3) are large cysteine-rich glycoproteins with multiple calcium binding epidermal growth factor (cbEGF)-like and TGFβ binding modules/8-cysteine (8-Cys) domains. In elastic tissues, fibrillins form a scaffold for tropoelastin deposition, facilitating the formation of **elastic fibers** with a hydrophobic elastin core and a surrounding microfibrillar coat (Fig. 10.2). Distinct tissues can have profoundly different elastic fiber organizations that\n\n<!-- p.197 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0198_01.jpg)\n\n\n**• Fig 10.2** Microfibril and elastic fiber assembly. (A) Following secretion of fibrillin 1, its N- and C-termini are processed by furin and then interact homotypically; this leads to axial and lateral fibrillin 1 assembly to form microfibrils. Beads may arise from folding of terminal regions, and transglutaminase crosslinks provide stabilization. Fibronectin is thought to act as a microfibril assembly template and/or to facilitate assembly at fibrillar adhesions by stimulating cytoskeletal tension through α5β1 integrin. Fibrillin 1 interacts with α5β1, αv β3, and αv β6 integrins during microfibril assembly. Heparan sulfate proteoglycans (HSPGs) are thought to facilitate cell surface–fibrillin 1 interactions, and heparin inhibits microfibril assembly. Fibrillin 1 also binds microfibril-associated glycoproteins, tropoelastin, fibulins, and latent transforming growth factor (TGF)-β binding protein. (B) Elastic fiber formation includes pericellular elastin “microassembly” and subsequent “macroassembly” on a microfibrillar scaffold. Secreted tropoelastin forms globules at the cell surface, which become crosslinked by lysyl oxidase (LOX); this process may involve αv β3 integrin interactions with tropoelastin, as well as interactions of HSPGs with integrins and tropoelastin. Fibulin 4 and fibulin 5 contribute to elastin crosslinking by lysyl oxidase and are also thought to direct the deposition of elastin globules onto preformed fibrillin-containing microfibrils, thereby forming elastic fibers. Microfibrils and elastic fibers are important matrix storage sites for bone morphogenic proteins (BMPs) and latent TGF-β1. (Figure 3: Adapted from Baldwin AK, Simpson A, Steer R, et al. Elastic fibres in health and disease. _Expert Rev Mol Med_ . 2013;15:e8.) \n\nreflect their specific functions. For example, in elastic arteries the elastic fibers are arranged in concentric lamellae, whereas in lung alveoli, the elastic fibers form networks that provide elasticity during breathing. \n\nMicrofibrils and elastic fibers are designed to maintain their elastic function over the entire lifespan. Loss of connective tissue elasticity is largely attributable to degeneration of the microfibrillar and elastic fiber network. Degradation of these extracellular networks is a major contributing factor in blood vessel aging, in the development of aortic aneurysms, in lung emphysema, and in degenerative changes in sundamaged skin. Fibrillin molecules and microfibrils are susceptible to degradation by various MMPs and serine proteinases; cumulative proteolytic damage is a major mechanism underlying degenerative changes in aging. Pathogenic defects in fibrillin-1 most frequently result in **Marfan syndrome** , but can also result in other rarer phenotypes, \n\nsuch as **Weill-Marchesani syndrome** , **stiff skin syndrome** , **geleophysic dysplasia** (see Chapter 11), or **acromelic dysplasia** (see Chapter 11). Defects in fibrillin-2 result in **congenital contractural arachnodactyly** , or more rarely in acromelic dysplasia, while defects in elastin lead to a form of **cutis laxa** . \n\n# **Proteoglycans** \n\nThe resiliency to compression of connective tissues such as cartilage or the aorta is largely explained by the presence of **proteoglycans** . Proteoglycans are found ubiquitously in the ECM, on cell surfaces, or in intracellular granules and are involved in a wide range of functions such as cell-cell communication, cell-matrix interactions, and cell growth and differentiation; they additionally interact with many ECM components, including collagens. Proteoglycans consist of a\n\n<!-- p.198 -->\n\ncore protein covalently attached to a large series of negatively charged linear polymers of disaccharides called **glycosaminoglycans (GAGs)** . GAGs are composed of repeating disaccharides of an amino sugar (N-acetyl-D-glucosamine or N-acetyl-D-galactosamine) and a type of uronic acid (D-glucuronic or L-iduronic acid). Two groups of GAGs are distinguished: _sulfated_ (e.g., chondroitin sulfate, dermatan sulfate, keratan sulfate, heparan sulfate) and _non-sulfated_ (e.g., heparin and hyaluronan), with sulfation determining the nature of interactions with other macromolecules such as cytokines and growth factors. At least 30 proteoglycans have been identified. The major proteoglycan of cartilage, **aggrecan** , has a core protein of 2,000 amino acids that is decorated with approximately 100 side chains of chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate. The core protein in turn binds to long chains of the polymeric disaccharide hyaluronan to form proteoglycan aggregates, one of the largest soluble macromolecular structures in nature. Because of its highly negative charge and extended structure, the aggrecan binds large amounts of water and small ions to distend the three-dimensional network of collagen fibers found in the same tissues, making the tissue resilient to pressure. Pathogenic variants in the gene coding for aggrecan _(ACAN)_ result in rare syndromic or non-syndromic forms of short stature that can be associated with early-onset osteoarthritis, early-onset lumbar disc degeneration, and mild dysmorphic features (Table 10.3). \n\nThe **small leucine-rich proteoglycans** (e.g., decorin and biglycan), the largest family of proteoglycans, are essential for correct collagen fibril formation, as different stages of collagen fibrillogenesis require different small leucine-rich proteoglycans. \n\n# **Glycoproteins** \n\nNumerous **glycoproteins** perform essential functions in ECM organization. **Fibronectin** , a major ECM glycoprotein, is a multidomain protein with the unique ability to bind many ECM molecules including collagens, signaling molecules, and cell adhesion molecules. Fibronectin mainly exists as a dimeric glycoprotein and organizes into a fibrillar network. Fibronectin fibers can undergo conformational changes and regulate the mechanical properties of tissues. \n\nThe **tenascin** family of glycoproteins interacts with many partners both in the ECM and on cell surfaces and are known to modulate ECM assembly and cell adhesion. There are four vertebrate tenascin paralogs (TN-C, TN-R, TN-W, and TN-X). TN-X is the largest member of the tenascin family and interacts with several ECM components directly and indirectly, including fibrillar collagens type I, III, and V; collagen types VI, XII, and XIV; and the small leucine-rich proteoglycan decorin. TN-X is involved in the organization of ECM architecture and tissue integrity as a regulator of collagen fibrillogenesis. \n\nThe **latent TGF** β-binding proteins (LTBPs) are extracellular glycoproteins that are classified as members of the fibrillin superfamily because they share a similar modular \n\ndomain structure, including tandem arrays of cbEGF domains that are interspersed with TGFβ binding modules/ 8-Cys type domains. There are four LTBPs, with LTBP-1, LTBP-3, and LTBP-4 having important roles in the processing and secretion of TGFβ. All four LTBP isoforms associate with fibrillin microfibrils. Because LTBPs were discovered as part of a large latent complex with TGFβ and its pro-peptide, they were given the name _latent TGF_ β binding proteins. In the endoplasmic reticulum, pro-TGFβ dimerizes and is disulfide bonded to LTBP to form a ternary complex. The TGFβ dimer is cleaved from its pro-peptide, latency-associated peptide (LAP), in the trans-Golgi network, but TGFβ and LAP remain strongly associated via noncovalent interactions forming the large latent complex (LLC). Once secreted, the LTBPs bind various matrix fibers, including fibrillin and fibronectin fibers that sequester latent TGFβ until it is released by an activator. \n\nThe **fibulins** are a multifunctional family of ECM glycoproteins comprising both long (fibulin-1, fibulin-2, and fibulin-6) and short fibulins (fibulin-3, fibulin-4, fibulin-5, and fibulin-7). Fibulins 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 bind tropoelastin and fibulins 2, 3, 4, and 5 are involved in elastic fiber formation. Fibulin-4 and fibulin-5 play important roles in elastogenesis. \n\n**Integrins** are arguably the most important cell surface receptors, anchoring the cytoskeleton to the ECM and functioning as crucial bidirectional signaling receptors between the intracellular and ECM environments. \n\n# **Heritable Connective Tissue Disorders** \n\nHeritable connective tissue disorders (HCTD) are caused by pathogenic genetic variants that perturb ECM assembly or homeostasis. Although individually rare, they collectively represent a significant proportion of human genetic disease. HCTDs affect mainly the skin, the eyes, and the musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, and pulmonary systems and are associated with significant morbidity and mortality. Prominent HCTDs include the **Ehlers-Danlos syndromes (EDS)** , **Marfan syndrome (MFS)** , and related conditions such as **Loeys-Dietz syndrome (LDS)** , the **cutis laxa syndromes** , and the skeletal dysplasias (see Chapter 11), including osteogenesis imperfecta. The chronicity and severity of HCTDs, combined with their hereditary nature, cause a significant burden on the quality of life of affected individuals and their families. \n\nHeritable connective tissue disorders arise from pathogenic variants in genes encoding ECM molecules or molecules related to matrix biosynthesis and cell signaling (Fig. 10.1). Clinically and genetically distinct HCTDs share phenotypic features because the causative pathogenic variants affect molecules that are expressed in overlapping tissues. Similar joint manifestations such as hypermobility are seen in the Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, Marfan syndrome, LoeysDietz syndrome, and the skeletal dysplasias because the genes associated with each of these diseases are all expressed in joint tissue (in addition to other tissues). Furthermore,\n\n<!-- p.199 -->\n\ndefects in a single connective tissue component usually manifest in multiple organs of the body, a concept known as **pleiotropy** , a universal feature of HCTDs. Most if not all HCTDs show full penetrance but are characterized by **variable expression** , wherein varying degrees of severity are seen in different individuals, including members of the same family that have the same pathogenic variant. The effects of pleiotropy and variable expression result in highly variable, clinically overlapping, multisystemic findings, which makes the phenotypic diagnosis of a specific disease entity challenging. Since HCTDs are genetically heterogeneous, prevalence ranges from rather commonly occurring autosomal dominant disorders to extremely rare recessively inherited conditions. Determining the true incidence of HCTDs is challenging because it relies on recognition of the disorders, which is biased toward their more severe, and therefore recognizable, phenotypes; less severely affected individuals may not be formally diagnosed, resulting in an underestimation of prevalence. \n\nThe clinical and molecular classification of HCTDs is dynamic and complex. Clinical classification is challenged by the variable expression of phenotypic features within a specific type of HCTD, as well as by the overlap of features among different HCTDs, making the delineation of distinct clinical phenotypes challenging. Molecular classification is challenged by genetic heterogeneity. Multiple genes can be associated with a single clinical phenotype, as seen in Loeys-Dietz syndrome, while different pathogenic variants \n\nin the same gene can lead to multiple clinical phenotypes, as seen with the genes coding for type I collagen, which can be associated with osteogenesis imperfecta, certain types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and Caffey disease. While molecular genetics provides a more precise and objective approach, clinical classification remains crucial for guiding initial assessments and subsequent care. \n\n## **The Ehlers-Danlos Syndromes** \n\nThe Ehlers-Danlos syndromes (EDS) are genetically heterogeneous heritable conditions which share several phenotypic features such as soft and hyperextensible skin, abnormal wound healing, easy bruising, and joint hypermobility (Fig. 10.3). Variable findings that differ among EDS subtypes include fragility of soft tissues, blood vessels, and hollow organs, as well as involvement of the musculoskeletal and ocular systems. Pathogenic variants in genes coding for the fibrillar collagens type III and V and, to a lesser extent, type I are found in many individuals with autosomal dominantinherited forms of EDS; in the rare autosomal recessive forms of EDS, pathogenic variants in other genes are at fault. A unifying histopathological pattern, seen to a variable extent across all EDS types, is disrupted biosynthesis and supramolecular organization of collagen fibrils in affected tissues. An overview of the different types of EDS, their molecular basis, inheritance patterns, and main clinical characteristics is given in Table 10.4. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0200_06.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.3** Clinical features of Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (EDS). Patients with classical EDS demonstrating hyperextensible skin (A), joint hypermobility at the wrist (B), atrophic horseshoe-shaped scars on the shin (C), piezogenic papules on the heel (D), and the Gorlin sign (E). (F) Translucent skin with visible veins on the chest in a patient with vascular EDS. (From Burrows NP, Ringpfeil F, Uillo J. Heritable disorders of connective tissue. In: Bolognia JL, Schaffer JV, Cerroni L, eds. _Dermatology_ . 5th ed., Vol. 2. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025 [Fig. 97.2, p. 1717].) \n\n_(Text continued on page 184)_\n\n<!-- p.200 -->\n\n## **TABLE Overview of Different Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) Types, Associated Genes, and Clinical Hallmarks With Major and Minor Criteria as Defined 10.4 in the International EDS Consortium Classification** \n\n||||Clinical Hallmarks|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|EDS Type<br>(Abbreviation)|Gene (Protein)|Major Clinical Criteria|Minor Clinical Criteria|\n|**Disorders of Collage **|**n Primary Structure an**|**d Collagen Processing**||\n|Classical (cEDS)*|_COL5A1_(α1(V)<br>procollagen chain)<br>_COL5A2_(α2(V)<br>procollagen chain)<br>_COL1A1_((α1(I)<br>procollagen chain:<br>p.(Arg312Cys) (rare)))|• Skin hyperextensibility with atrophic<br>scarring<br>• Generalized joint hypermobility|• Easy bruising<br>• Soft doughy skin<br>• Skin fragility (or traumatic splitting)<br>• Molluscoid pseudotumors (bluish-gray, spongy nodules, which are<br>herniations of subcutaneous fat, seen over easily traumatized areas)<br>• Subcutaneous spheroids<br>• Hernia (or history thereof)<br>• Epicanthal folds<br>• Complications of joint hypermobility (e.g., sprain, (sub)luxation, pain,<br>flexible flatfoot)<br>• Family history of a first-degree relative who meets criteria|\n|Vascular (vEDS)*|_COL3A1_(α1(III)<br>procollagen chain)<br>_COL1A1_(α1(I)<br>procollagen chain: p.<br>(Arg312Cys) (rare)<br>p.(Arg574Cys) (rare)<br>p.(Arg1093Cys) (rare))|• Family history of vEDS with documented<br>pathogenic variant in COL3A1<br>• Arterial rupture at young age<br>• Spontaneous sigmoid colon perforation in<br>the absence of known colon disease<br>• Uterine rupture during third trimester of<br>pregnancy<br>• Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula (in the<br>absence of trauma)|• Bruising unrelated to identified trauma and/or in unusual sites such as<br>cheeks and back<br>• Thin, translucent skin with increased venous visibility<br>• Characteristic facial features<br>• Spontaneous pneumothorax<br>• Acrogeria<br>• Talipes equinovarus<br>• Congenital hip dislocation<br>• Small joint hypermobility<br>• Tendon and muscle rupture<br>• Gingival recession and gingival fragility<br>• Early-onset varicose veins|\n|Arthrochalasia (aEDS)*|_COL1A1_(α1(I)<br>procollagen chain)<br>_COL1A2_(α2(I)<br>procollagen chain)|• Congenital bilateral hip dislocation<br>• Severe generalized joint hypermobility with<br>multiple dislocations<br>• Skin hyperextensibility|• Muscle hypotonia<br>• Kyphoscoliosis<br>• Radiologically mild osteopenia<br>• Tissue fragility, including atrophic scars<br>• Easy bruising|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.201 -->\n\n## **TABLE Overview of Different Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) Types, Associated Genes, and Clinical Hallmarks With Major and Minor Criteria as Defined 10.4 in the International EDS Consortium Classification—cont’d** \n\n|EDS Type<br>(Abbreviation)|Gene (Protein)|Major Clinical Criteria|Clinical Hallmarks<br>Minor Clinical Criteria|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Dermatosparaxis<br>(dEDS)<sup>†</sup>|_ADAMTS2_(ADAMTS2,<br>N-proteinase)|• Extreme skin fragility with congenital or<br>postnatal tears<br>• Craniofacial features: large fontanelle, puffy<br>eyelids, excessive periorbital skin, down-<br>slanting palpebral fissures, blue sclerae,<br>hypoplastic chin<br>• Progressively redundant, almost lax skin<br>with excessive skin folds at wrists and<br>ankles<br>• Increased palmar wrinkling<br>• Severe bruisability with risk of<br>subcutaneous hematoma<br>• Umbilical hernia<br>• Postnatal growth retardation with short<br>limbs<br>• Perinatal complications related to tissue<br>fragility|• Soft and doughy skin texture<br>• Skin hyperextensibility<br>• Atrophic scars<br>• Generalized joint hypermobility<br>• Complications of visceral fragility (e.g., rectal prolapse, bladder or<br>diaphragm rupture)<br>• Delayed motor development<br>• Osteopenia<br>• Hirsutism<br>• Tooth abnormalities<br>• Refractive errors<br>• Strabismus|\n|Cardiac-valvular<br>(cvEDS)<sup>†</sup>|_COL1A2_(α2(V)<br>procollagen chain (total<br>absence))|• Severe progressive cardiac-valvular<br>insufficiency<br>• Skin involvement<br>• Joint hypermobility (generalized or<br>restricted to small joints|• Inguinal hernia<br>• Pectus deformity<br>• Joint dislocations<br>• Foot deformities: pes planus, pes planovalgus, hallux valgus|\n|**Disorders of Collage **|**n Folding and Collagen**|**Crosslinking**||\n|Kyphoscoliotic (kEDS-<br>_PLOD1_or kEDS-<br>_FKBP14_depending<br>on the causative<br>pathogenic variant)<sup>†</sup>|_PLOD1_(Lysylhydroxylase 1)<br>_FKBP14_(FKBP22)|• Congenital muscle hypotonia<br>• Congenital or early onset kyphoscoliosis<br>• Generalized joint hypermobility with (sub)<br>luxations|• Skin hyperextensibility<br>• Easy bruising<br>• Rupture/aneurysm of medium-sized artery<br>• Osteopenia/osteoporosis<br>• Blue sclerae<br>• Umbilical or inguinal hernia<br>• Pectus deformity<br>• Marfanoid habitus<br>• Talipes equinovarus<br>• Refractive errors<br>_PLOD1_<br>• Skin fragility<br>• Microcornea<br>• Characteristics craniofacial features<br>_FKBP14_<br>• Congenital hearing impairment<br>• Muscle atrophy<br>• Bladder diverticula|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.202 -->\n\n## **TABLE Overview of Different Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) Types, Associated Genes, and Clinical Hallmarks With Major and Minor Criteria as Defined 10.4 in the International EDS Consortium Classification—cont’d** \n\n||||Clinical Hallmarks|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|EDS Type<br>(Abbreviation)|Gene (Protein)|Major Clinical Criteria|Minor Clinical Criteria|\n|**Disorders of Structu**<br>|**re and Function of the My**<br>|**omatrix**<br>||\n|Classical-like (clEDS)<sup>†</sup>|_TNXB_(Tenascin X)|• Skin hyperextensibility with velvety skin<br>texture and absence of atrophic scarring<br>• Generalized joint hypermobility<br>• Easy bruisable skin/spontaneous<br>ecchymoses|• Foot deformities<br>• Edema in legs in absence of cardiac failure<br>• Mild proximal and distal muscle weakness<br>• Axonal polyneuropathy<br>• Atrophy of muscle in hands and feet<br>• Acrogeric hands, mallet finger(s), clinoor brachydactyly<br>• Vaginal/uterine/rectal prolapse|\n|Myopathic(mEDS)<sup>‡</sup>|_COL12A1_(α1(XII)<br>procollagen chain)|• Congenital muscle hypotonia and/or<br>muscle atrophy<br>• Proximal joint contractures<br>• Hypermobility of distal joints|• Soft, doughy skin<br>• Atrophic scarring<br>• Motor developmental delay<br>• Myopathy on muscle biopsy|\n|**Disorders of Glycos**|**aminoglycan Biosynthesis**|||\n|Musculo-contractural<br>(mcEDS-_CHST14_or<br>mcEDS-_DES_<br>depending on the<br>causative<br>pathogenic variant)<sup>†</sup>|_CHST14_(Dermatan-4-_O_-<br>sulfotransferase-1)<br>_DSE_(Dermatan sulfate<br>epimerase-1)|• Congenital multiple contractures (typically<br>adduction/flexion contractures and talipes<br>equinovarus)<br>• Characteristic craniofacial features: large<br>fontanelle, short down-slanting palpebral<br>fissures, blue sclerae, hypertelorism, short<br>nose with hypoplastic columella, low-set<br>and rotated ears, long philtrum with thin<br>upper lip vermillion, small mouth and<br>hypoplastic chin<br>• Characteristic cutaneous features: skin<br>hyperextensibility, easy bruising, skin<br>fragility with atrophic scars Increased<br>palmar wrinkling|• Recurrent/chronic dislocations<br>• Pectus deformities<br>• Spinal deformities<br>• Peculiar fingers<br>• Progressive talipes deformities<br>• Large subcutaneous hematomas<br>• Chronic constipation<br>• Colonic diverticula<br>• Pneumo(hemo)thorax<br>• Nephrolithiasis/Cystolithiasis<br>• Hydronephrosis<br>• Cryptorchidism in males<br>• Strabismus<br>• Refractive errors<br>• Glaucoma|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.203 -->\n\n## **TABLE Overview of Different Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) Types, Associated Genes, and Clinical Hallmarks With Major and Minor Criteria as Defined 10.4 in the International EDS Consortium Classification—cont’d** \n\n|EDS Type<br>(Abbreviation)|Gene (Protein)|Major Clinical Criteria|Clinical Hallmarks<br>Minor Clinical Criteria|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Spondylodysplastic<br>(spEDS-_B4GALT7_or<br>spEDS-_B3GALT6_<br>depending on the<br>causative<br>pathogenic variant)<sup>†</sup>|<br>_B4GALT7_<br>(Galactosyltransferase I)<br>_B3GALT6_<br>(Galactosyltransferase II)|• Short stature (progressive in childhood)<br>• Muscle hypotonia (ranging from severe<br>congenital to mild later-onset)<br>• Bowing of limbs|• Skin hyperextensibility, soft and doughy, thin and translucent skin<br>• Pes planus<br>• Delayed motor development<br>• Osteopenia<br>• Delayed cognitive impairment<br>_B4GALT7_<br>• Radioulnar synostosis<br>• Bilateral elbow contractures<br>• Single transverse palmar crease<br>• Characteristic craniofacial features<br>• Characteristic X-ray findings of skeletal dysplasia<br>• Clouded cornea<br>_B3GALT6_<br>• Kyphoscoliosis (congenital or early onset)<br>• Joint hypermobility (generalized or restricted to distal joints)<br>• Joint contractures (congenital or progressive)<br>• Peculiar fingers<br>• Characteristic craniofacial features<br>• Tooth discoloration, dysplastic teeth<br>• Characteristic X-ray findings of skeletal dysplasia<br>• Osteoporosis with spontaneous fractures<br>• Aortic aneurysm<br>• Lung hypoplasia, restrictive lung disease|\n|**Disorders of Intrace**|**llular Processes**|||\n|Spondylodysplastic<br>(spEDS)<sup>†</sup>|_SLC39A13_(ZIP13)|• Short stature (progressive in childhood)<br>• Muscle hypotonia (ranging from severe<br>congenital to mild later-onset)<br>• Bowing of limbs|• Skin hyperextensibility, soft and doughy, thin and translucent skin<br>• Pes planus<br>• Delayed motor development<br>• Osteopenia<br>• Delayed cognitive impairment<br>• Protuberant eyes with bluish sclerae<br>• Hands with finely wrinkled palms<br>• Skeletal dysplasia<br>• Atrophy of thenar muscles and tapering fingers<br>• Hypermobility of distal joints<br>• Characteristic X-ray findings of skeletal dysplasia|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.204 -->\n\n## **TABLE Overview of Different Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) Types, Associated Genes, and Clinical Hallmarks With Major and Minor Criteria as Defined 10.4 in the International EDS Consortium Classification—cont’d** \n\n|EDS Type<br>(Abbreviation)|Gene (Protein)|Major Clinical Criteria|Clinical Hallmarks<br>Minor Clinical Criteria|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Brittle Cornea<br>Syndrome (BCS)<sup>†</sup>|_ZNF469_(ZNF469)<br>_PRDM5_(PRDM5)|• Thin cornea with/without rupture<br>• Early-onset progressive keratoconus and/<br>or keratoglobus<br>• Blue sclerae|• Enucleation or corneal scarring as a result of previous rupture<br>• Progressive loss of corneal stromal depth<br>• High myopia<br>• Retinal detachment<br>• Deafness (often mixed conductive and sensorineural)<br>• Hypercompliant tympanic membranes<br>• Developmental dysplasia of hip<br>• Hypotonia in infancy (usually mild)<br>• Scoliosis<br>• Arachnodactyly<br>• Hypermobility of distal joints<br>• Pes planus, hallux valgus<br>• Mild finger contractures<br>• Soft, velvety and/or translucent skin|\n|**Disorders of Compl **|**ement Pathway**|||\n|Periodontal (pEDS)*|_C1R_(C1r)<br>_C1S_(C1s)|• Severe and intractable early-onset<br>periodontitis<br>• Lack of attached gingiva<br>• Pretibial plaques<br>• Family history of first degree relative who<br>meets clinical criteria|• Easy bruising<br>• Joint hypermobility, mostly distal<br>• Skin hyperextensibility and fragility, wide or atrophic scarring<br>• Increased infection rate<br>• Hernias<br>• Marfanoid facial features<br>• Acrogeria<br>• Prominent vasculature|\n|**Molecularly Unresol**|**ved Forms of EDS**|||\n|Hypermobile (hEDS)*|Unknown|||\n|**Additional EDS Vari**|**ants**|||\n|(Classical-like type 2)<br>(provisional) (clEDS)<sup>†</sup><br>Of note, the consortium spei                    i<br>pathophysiologic mechanism<br>*Denotes autosomal domina<br>†Denotes autosomal recessi<br>‡Denotes autosomal domina<br>Adapted from Malfait F, Fran         i|_AEBP1_(ACLP)<br>cifies 13 types, though in this ta              i<br>s.<br>nt inheritance.<br>ve inheritance.<br>nt or autosomal recessive inher<br>comano C, Byers P, et al. The 2  i|• Skin hyperextensibility with atrophic scarring<br>• Generalized joint hypermobility<br>• Foot deformities<br>• Early-onset osteopenia<br>i      ble, two separate entries were created for spondylodysplastic ED      i<br>itance.<br>017 international classification of the Ehlers-Danlos syndromes.|i              S and classical-like EDS to highlight specific genotype-phenotype correlations and underlying<br>i_Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet_. 2017;175(1):8-26|\n\n<!-- p.205 -->\n\n# **Molecular Basis** \n\n**Classical EDS** and **vascular EDS** are two of the more common autosomal dominant forms of EDS. Classical EDS is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the genes coding for type V collagen ( _COL5A1_ and _COL5A2_ ), while vascular EDS is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the gene coding for type III collagen _(COL3A1)_ . A rare arginine to cysteine substitution in _COL1A1_ (c.934C>T; p.Arg312Cys) and a range of glutamic acid to lysine substitutions in _COL3A1_ are associated with a phenotype that combines features of classical and vascular EDS. \n\nBiallelic _COL1A2_ pathogenic variants that result in a complete absence of α2 chains in type I collagen give rise to the very rare autosomal recessive **cardiac-valvular EDS** . **Arthrochalasia EDS** and **dermatosparaxis EDS** both result from deficient cleavage of the N-pro-peptide of type I procollagen. In arthrochalasia EDS this deficient cleavage is due to heterozygous pathogenic variants in either _COL1A1_ or _COL1A2_ that result in loss of the cleavage site, whereas dermatosparaxis (“tearing of the skin”) EDS is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the gene that codes for the procollagen N-proteinase (ADAMTS2). The persistence of the N-pro-peptide disturbs fibrillogenesis, resulting in collagen fibrils that are thin and irregular. \n\nA phenotype that couples EDS with signs of moderate to severe _myopathy_ is related to heterozygous or homozygous pathogenic variants in _COL12A1_ , which codes for type XII collagen, a FACIT collagen. **Kyphoscoliotic EDS** is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in either _PLOD1_ , which encodes procollagen-lysine 5-dioxygenase (lysyl hydroxylase 1), an enzyme required for formation of stable crosslinks in collagen fibers, or in _FKBP14_ , which encodes FKBP22, an endoplasmic reticulum resident molecular chaperone that acts as a quality control on the folded triple helix of type III and type VI collagen. Some individuals with clinical characteristics resembling those of classical EDS have biallelic pathogenic variants in either _TNXB_ , encoding tenascin-X, or in _AEBP1_ , which encodes the ECM-associated adipocyte enhancer-binding protein (AEBP1), which assists in collagen polymerization. **Spondylodysplastic EDS** is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in _B3GALT7_ , which encodes galactosyltransferase I, or in _B3GALT6_ , which encodes galactosyltransferase II, both key enzymes in the biosynthesis of the linker region of GAGs. **Musculocontractural EDS** results from pathogenic variants in genes encoding enzymes responsible for dermatan biosynthesis: _CHST14_ , dermatan 4-O-sulfotransferase 1, and _DSE_ , dermatan sulfate epimerase. A rare type of **spondylodysplastic EDS** is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in _SLC39A13_ , encoding the intracellular zinc transporter ZIP13. \n\n**Brittle cornea syndrome** is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in either _ZNF469_ or in _PRDM5_ , both putative transcriptional regulators. **Periodontic EDS** is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in _C1R_ or _C1S_ , which encode the complement pathway components C1q and C1s, respectively. A small percentage of individuals with one \n\nof these genetically elucidated EDS phenotypes, or EDS phenotypes that cannot unequivocally be put in one specific clinical category, have no identifiable genetic defect despite rigorous genetic testing. In addition, the molecular underpinnings of hypermobile EDS (hEDS) remain elusive, and hEDS diagnosis is based on clinical criteria and exclusion of other conditions. \n\n# **Clinical Manifestations** \n\nThe defining hallmarks of EDS across all types are the combined presence of skin hyperextensibility and fragility, and joint hypermobility that is usually but not always generalized. \n\n# **_Skin_** \n\nOne of the principal features of EDS is _skin hyperextensibility_ , referring to a stretchy skin that snaps back after release (Fig. 10.3). The skin often has a smooth, soft, or velvety feel, and can be thin and translucent. The skin is fragile and tears easily, even after minor trauma, and heals slowly. Widened and thin scars, also known as “atrophic” or “cigarette paper” scars, are frequently observed in different types of EDS (Fig. 10.4). These scars are more frequently observed over pressure points and exposed areas such as the forehead, elbows, knees, and shins. Individuals with vascular EDS usually do not have a velvety hyperextensible skin, but skin can be thin and translucent with visible superficial veins. _Easy_ \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0206_11.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.4** Atrophic scarring in an individual with classical EhlersDanlos syndrome.\n\n<!-- p.206 -->\n\n_bruising_ is commonly seen in all types of EDS. In children with undiagnosed classical or vascular EDS, the presence of multiple bruises may elicit a suspicion of child maltreatment. Repetitive bruising causes discoloration of the skin due to deposition of hemosiderin, a phenomenon referred to as “hemosiderotic” scars that is more frequently observed in classical, vascular, and periodontal EDS. In more severe types, such as the autosomal recessive musculocontractural and classical-like EDS, a bleeding diathesis may manifest itself as severe spontaneous or recurring hematomas. \n\n# **_Joints_** \n\nThe presence of generalized _joint hypermobility_ is not diagnostic of EDS, despite being one of the most frequent \n\nindications in both children and adults to be referred to a specialist for consideration of EDS. Joint hypermobility is a continuous trait modified by age, sex, and ethnicity, as well as by environmental factors. The reported prevalence of generalized joint hypermobility in the general population is as high as 57% in females and 35% in males. In addition, joint hypermobility is also observed in many syndromic conditions other than EDS. In EDS, joint hypermobility is variable in severity and is usually but not always generalized. Joint hypermobility is typically assessed using the 9-point **Beighton scale** (Fig. 10.5). Hypermobility is defined as generalized if the score is ≥6 for prepubertal children and adolescents, ≥5 for post-pubertal males and females until age 50 years, and ≥4 for males and \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0207_06.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.5** Beighton score. The range of motion of several key small and large joints is measured to provide an overview of joint hypermobility. Instability is not assessed. Scoring: 2 points for each bilateral measure in Nos. 1 to 4 and 1 point for No. 5, equaling a total possible score of 9. Hypermobility is considered significant with a score of ≥6 between the ages of 6 and 35.  (Modified from Smits-Engelsman B, Klerks M, Kirby A. Beighton score: a valid measure for generalized hypermobility in children. _J Pediatr_ . 2011;158:119-123.e4.)\n\n<!-- p.207 -->\n\nfemales over 50 years of age. This Beighton scale, while easy to use in daily clinical practice, has major limitations, and should be considered more as a screening assessment. While often an asset to play or sports participation in childhood, joint hypermobility can become a serious burden over time, often complicated by repetitive subluxations, dislocations, sprains, and chronic joint pain that is difficult to treat. \n\n# **_Other Signs and Symptoms_** \n\nOther signs of generalized connective tissue weakness and fragility, as well as other musculoskeletal problems, can be observed to varying degrees and may help distinguish among different EDS types. Congenital **umbilical hernia** is commonly seen in dermatosparaxis EDS, whereas _later-onset repetitive_ **inguinal** and **umbilical hernias** are sometimes observed in other types of EDS. **Rupture of medium and large-sized arteries** is a major concern in vascular EDS but has been reported in a few other types as well, especially in classical and kyphoscoliotic EDS. Patients with vascular EDS are at increased risk for rupture of the gastrointestinal tract (especially the sigmoid colon), the gravid uterus, and other internal organs such as the spleen. Early-onset severe varicose veins, carotid-cavernous fistulae, and spontaneous pneumothorax are also features of vascular EDS. Cardiacvalvular defects and aortic root dilatation are _usually not_ part of the vascular EDS phenotype, but rather are restricted to some of the rarer autosomal recessive forms of EDS. Obstetrical and pelvic complications such as cervical insufficiency, premature rupture of membranes, vaginal \n\nlacerations, and organ prolapses (e.g., uterus, bladder, rectum) may occur. Prenatal ultrasound abnormalities are uncommon, with the exception of congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract in musculocontractural EDS. Sclerae may be blue, especially in arthrochalasia and dermatosparaxis EDS. Other **ophthalmological signs** and symptoms include microcornea and scleral rupture in kyphoscoliotic EDS; keratoconus, keratoglobus, and corneal rupture in brittle cornea syndrome; and corneal clouding in spondylodysplastic EDS. Keratoconus, a conical protrusion of the cornea (Fig. 10.6) has also been sporadically observed in vascular EDS patients. \n\n**Musculoskeletal features** include congenital bilateral hip dislocation, spine deformities (e.g., scoliosis, kyphosis), pectus deformities (e.g., pectus carinatum, pectus excavatum), talipes equinovarus, and in some rarer types (e.g., musculocontractural and spondylodysplastic EDS), a mild skeletal dysplasia. Muscle hypotonia is observed in a number of EDS types (notably classical, classical-like, myopathic, kyphoscoliotic, and musculocontractural EDS) and, in combination with joint laxity, may cause _floppy infant syndrome_ , or a delay in motor development. A combination of congenital proximal contractures with distal joint hypermobility is observed in a few EDS types, including myopathic, musculocontractural, and spondylodysplastic EDS. \n\n**Central nervous system** abnormalities are rare in EDS. Ventricular enlargement, Dandy-Walker anomaly, hypoplasia of the septum pellucidum, and hydrocephalus have been reported in musculocontractural and spondylodysplastic EDS. Intellectual disabilities and hearing loss are not \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0208_07.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.6** Keratoconus. (A) Unaffected eye, (B) eye with keratoconus-related conical protrusion, (C) comparison of unaffected cornea and cornea with keratoconus. (From Kaya H, Çavuşoğlu A, Çakmak HB, Şen B, Çalık E. Chapter 31: Keratoconus disease and three-dimensional simulation of the cornea throughout the process of cross-linking treatment. In: Tran QN, Arabnia H, eds. _Emerging Trends in Computer Science and Applied Computing, Emerging Trends in Computational Biology, Bioinformatics, and Systems Biology_ . Morgan Kaufmann; 2015:561-575.)\n\n<!-- p.208 -->\n\nconsidered part of the general EDS phenotype but have sporadically been reported in patients from consanguineous families with rare autosomal recessive forms of EDS; it is unclear whether this is truly part of the EDS phenotype in those patients or whether it is unrelated to EDS and is caused by other genetic factors. \n\nFacial dysmorphic features may help in the recognition of certain EDS types. In the autosomal dominant forms, these dysmorphisms are usually mild, while in the autosomal recessive types they are more pronounced. Vascular EDS is characterized by the presence of thin lips, narrow nose, attached ear lobes, large, prominent eyes, and potentially severe gingival recession (Fig. 10.7). Gingival recession should also raise concern for the periodontal form of EDS, in which early-onset periodontitis with extensive periodontal destruction and loss of teeth, starting in childhood or adolescence, is universally present. Individuals with classical EDS often \n\nhave epicanthal folds and large protruding ears. Individuals with dermatosparaxis EDS have a recognizable facial appearance with prominent and protuberant eyes; puffy, edematous eyelids and excessive periorbital skin; large fontanelles and wide cranial sutures; micrognathia; and gingival hyperplasia. Individuals with musculocontractural EDS present with large fontanelles, downslanting palpebral fissures, hypertelorism, a short nose with a hypoplastic columella, ear deformities, high-arched palate, long philtrum, thin upper lip vermilion, small mouth, and micro-retrognathia. \n\n# **Classification** \n\nThe 2017 International EDS Consortium classification defines 13 clinical EDS types that are caused by pathogenic variants in 20 different genes (Table 10.4) that are grouped into different categories based on the function of the encoded protein: \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0209_07.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.7** Illustration of the clinical spectrum of findings in individuals with vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome secondary to pathogenic variants in _COL3A1._ (From Pizano A, Tenorio ER, Tanaka A, Oderich G. Chapter 24: Aneurysmatic degeneration of connective tissue diseases: from diagnosis to treatment. In: _Vascular Surgery: A Guide to Clinical Decision-Making._ Elsevier; 2022:273-295.)\n\n<!-- p.209 -->\n\n(a) collagen structure and processing, (b) collagen folding and crosslinking, (c) the myomatrix, the interface between muscle and ECM, (d) glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, (e) miscellaneous intracellular processes, and (f) complement pathway. \n\naneurysm at the sinuses of Valsalva, with subsequent evolution to aortic dissection, are associated with high mortality rates, the first presenting symptoms of Marfan syndrome are often non-vascular and include skeletal, ophthalmologic, or pulmonary abnormalities. \n\n# **Epidemiology** \n\nThe incidence of all forms of EDS is ~1 in 5,000 individuals, with no apparent ethnic or sex predisposition. Individuals with milder symptoms frequently do not seek medical attention, leading to underestimation of incidence rates. Classical EDS, with an incidence of 1 in 20,000, and vascular EDS, with an incidence as high as 1 in 50,000 and as low as 1 in 200,000, are the most common genetically defined forms of EDS. For the other types of EDS for which genetic defects have been identified, there are no incidence estimates, but the number of identified individuals with each of these disorders is below 100. \n\nThe incidence of hEDS is not known. While generally considered to be the most common type of EDS, it exists in a phenotypic continuum with the **hypermobility spectrum disorders** , a group of conditions characterized by symptomatic joint hypermobility and a range of additional extraarticular signs and symptoms including fatigue, chronic pain, headaches, functional gastrointestinal disorders, and dizziness or brain fog. The incidence of hypermobility spectrum disorders is also unknown, though is felt to be common. Both conditions are more frequently diagnosed in females than in males. \n\n# **Marfan Syndrome and Related Disorders** \n\nMarfan syndrome is a common HCTD. While vascular complications, particularly the development of an aortic \n\n# **Molecular Basis** \n\nMost Marfan syndrome is caused by dominant negative and haploinsufficient pathogenic variants in _FBN1_ . Inheritance is therefore typically autosomal dominant, although in rare instances, biallelic variants have been identified. Approximately 70% of affected individuals have an affected parent; ~30% of affected individuals have a _de novo_ pathogenic variant. Over 2,000 different pathogenic variants in _FBN1_ have been described. Most are private (i.e., are variants with a low allele frequency, typically found within only a single family within a cohort) and are scattered throughout its 65 coding exons. About 10% are recurrent and are in CpG sequences known to be _hot spots_ . Approximately two-thirds of the variants in these hot spots are missense variants that alter calcium-binding domains in the repetitive EGF–like domains of the protein, and about one-third of the variants introduce premature termination codons (Fig. 10.8). Rarer variants alter the processing of the protein. The severity of the phenotype can usually not be predicted from the nature of the variant; however, pathogenic variants in exons 24–32 of _FBN1_ typically result in a severe phenotype associated with childhood mortality (previously called **neonatal Marfan syndrome** ). \n\nPathogenic variants in _FBN1_ produce the phenotypic manifestations of Marfan syndrome by weakening the structure of the ECM _and_ also through effects on TGFβ signaling \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0210_11.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.8** Schematic representation of fibrillin-1 gene and protein. _FBN1_ consists of 65 exons located on chromosome 15. It encodes fibrillin-1 (2,871 amino acids, 350 kDa), a multi-domain glycoprotein, integral for ECM stability. (From Ramachandra CJ, Mehta A, Guo KW, Wong P, Tan JL, Shim W. Molecular pathogenesis of Marfan syndrome. _Int J Cardiol_ . 2015;187:585-591.)\n\n<!-- p.210 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0211_01.jpg)\n\n\n(Fig. 10.9); phenotypically related disorders such as LoeysDietz syndrome have features that overlap with Marfan syndrome related to disruptions in TGFβ pathways. Some pathogenic variants in _FBN1_ produce alterations in binding sites that modulate TGFβ bioavailability during development of the skeleton and other tissues. In aortic tissues, reduced or altered forms of fibrillin-1 stimulate the release of sequestered TGFβ and increase its activity, resulting in altered transcription of target genes, including connective tissue growth factor and matrix metalloproteinases (MMP-2 and MMP-9). The aortic phenotype is caused by vascular remodeling due to a combination of structural microfibril changes, excess TGFβ, and overexpression of MMP-2 and MMP-9. \n\n# **Epidemiology** \n\nWith an incidence of ~1 in 5,000, Marfan syndrome is one of the most common HCTDs, occurring worldwide without ethnic or sex predisposition. \n\n# **Clinical Manifestations** \n\nMarfan syndrome is most frequently associated with cardiovascular, ocular, and musculoskeletal abnormalities, but \n\nlung, skin, and central nervous system involvement may also occur. Decreased life expectancy is primarily due to aortic complications including aortic root dilatation and dissection. \n\n# **_Cardiovascular_** \n\n_Dilatation of the aortic root at the sinuses of Valsalva_ is a characteristic and ominous feature of Marfan syndrome that can develop at any age. The rate of dilatation is unpredictable. Undiagnosed and untreated Marfan syndrome is associated with **aortic dissection** that occurs when the maximal dimension reaches approximately 5 cm. Dissection typically begins above the coronary ostia and can extend the entire length of the aorta. Aortic dissection is exceedingly rare in early childhood. Dilatation may also involve other segments of the thoracic aorta, the abdominal aorta, the root of the pulmonary artery, or even the carotid and intracranial arteries. **Mitral valve prolapse** is observed in ~50% of individuals with Marfan syndrome and is more prevalent in women; its frequency increases with age. Heart failure caused by prolapse and mitral valve regurgitation represents a major source of morbidity and mortality in young children with rapidly progressive Marfan syndrome. Additional cardiac findings may include cardiomyopathy with biventricular enlargement and mild systolic dysfunction. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0211_10.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.9** Schematic representation of extracellular matrix disintegration in Marfan syndrome. Fibrillin-1 aggregates into functional microfibrils and sequesters TGF-β in the ECM, regulating its signaling. Loss of fibrillin-1 in Marfan syndrome decreases or eliminates this TGF-β sequestration, and its constitutive stimulation activates genes like MMPs, which induce elastolysis, decreasing ECM stability. (From Ramachandra CJ, Mehta A, Guo KW, Wong P, Tan JL, Shim W. Molecular pathogenesis of Marfan syndrome. _Int J Cardiol_ . 2015;187:585-591.)\n\n<!-- p.211 -->\n\n# **_Musculoskeletal_** \n\nAffected individuals typically display a _marfanoid habitus_ with tall stature and long limbs (Fig. 10.10). The ratio of the upper segment (i.e., top of the head to the top of the pubic ramus) to the lower segment (i.e., top of the pubic ramus to the floor) is usually 2 standard deviations below mean for age, ethnicity, and sex. The fingers and hands are long and slender and have a spider-like appearance termed **arachnodactyly** . Other musculoskeletal manifestations include joint hypermobility, reduced elbow extension, anterior chest deformities (e.g., pectus excavatum, pectus carinatum), vertebral column deformity (e.g., scoliosis and thoracolumbar kyphosis), maxillary overbite, protrusion acetabula (which can lead to osteoarthritis), and hind foot deformity (e.g., pes planus). \n\n# **_Ophthalmologic_** \n\nMyopia is the most common ocular feature, affecting over 50% of individuals, often presenting in early childhood. **Ectopia lentis** , displacement of the lens from the center of the pupil, occurs in about 60% of individuals. The ocular globe is frequently elongated and the cornea may be flat; early cataract formation and glaucoma can occur. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0212_05.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.10** Marfan syndrome. Note the long slim limbs, pectus excavatum, narrow face, and reduced elbow extension. (From Jones KL, Jones MC, Del Campo M. _Smith’s Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation_ . 8th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022: Fig. 1A, p. 662.) \n\n# **_Other Signs and Symptoms_** \n\n**Craniofacial** characteristics of MFS include dolichocephaly, exophthalmos, downslanting palpebral fissures, malar hypoplasia, high arched palate with tooth crowding, and micrognathia or retrognathia. **Mucocutaneous** features include striae, typically over the shoulders and buttocks (Fig. 10.11), and inguinal and incisional hernias. The skin is not hyperextensible, in contrast to EDS. **Pulmonary** blebs are a frequent feature and evolve toward spontaneous pneumothorax. Obstructive and central sleep apnea is observed more frequently than in the general population and is related to increased risk of atrial fibrillation, decreased left ventricular ejection fraction, aortic events, and complications of mitral valve surgery. **Neurological** findings include dural ectasia, as well as spinal arachnoid cysts or diverticula. Severe dural ectasia, including meningoceles, are sometimes accompanied by neurological signs due to pressure on the nerves. \n\n# **Related Disorders** \n\n**Loeys-Dietz syndrome (LDS)** is a group of autosomal dominantly inherited disorders that have considerable phenotypic similarities with Marfan syndrome. Overlapping craniofacial features include elongated facies, downslanting palpebral fissures, highly arched palate, malar hypoplasia, micrognathia, and retrognathia. Additional similar features include pectus deformities, scoliosis, pes planus, arachnodactyly, joint laxity, dural ectasia, spontaneous pneumothorax, and aortic root aneurysm with dissection. When presenting with typical features, LDS can be _distinguished_ from Marfan syndrome by the unique presence of hypertelorism, an abnormal uvula (typically broad with raphe or bifid) or cleft palate, and widespread aortic and arterial aneurysms and tortuosity, with the head and neck arteries being the primary sites of arterial involvement. Individuals with LDS most often present with cardiovascular features first, while this is not the case for Marfan syndrome. Aortic aneurysms in LDS behave very differently from those in Marfan syndrome, with dissection and rupture occurring more frequently, at smaller dimensions, and earlier in life, including during early childhood. Additional features that distinguish LDS from Marfan syndrome include craniosynostosis, hydrocephalus (a rare finding), Chiari I malformation, joint contractures, and cervical spine instability. Furthermore, talipes equinovarus; inguinal, umbilical, and hiatal hernias; blue sclerae; thin, translucent skin; poor wound healing; atrophic scars; and easy bruising are also frequently observed in LDS though not in Marfan syndrome. Many of these features are observed in vascular EDS; as such, LDS is an important differential diagnosis of vascular EDS. \n\nLoeys-Dietz syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in genes encoding components of the TGFβ signaling pathway, including the cytokines TGFβ2 and TGFβ3, the cytokine receptors TGFBR1 and TGFBR2, and the downstream effectors SMAD2, SMAD3. These loss-of-function variants all lead to increases in TGFβ signaling.\n\n<!-- p.212 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0213_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.11** Marfan syndrome. Note the joint laxity (A), Steinberg thumb sign (B), ability to join thumb and fifth finger around the wrist (Walker-Murdoch sign) (C), pes planus (D), and striae over hips and back (E). (From Jones KL, Jones MC, _Del Campo M. Smith’s Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation_ . 8th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022; Fig. 2, p. 664.) \n\n**Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (CCA)** , also referred to as **Beals–Hecht syndrome** or **distal arthrogryposis type 9** , is an autosomal dominant disorder with phenotypic features similar to Marfan syndrome. CCA is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in _FBN2_ , the gene encoding fibrillin-2, and is characterized by tall stature, arachnodactyly, progressive scoliosis, contractures, and crumpled ears. Cardiovascular abnormalities include mitral valve prolapse and aortic root dilatation, though aortic dissection is rare. Congenital cardiac and gastrointestinal anomalies requiring surgical repair in early infancy have also been reported. The differential diagnosis of Marfan syndrome is presented in Table 10.5. \n\n## **Cutis Laxa Syndromes** \n\nThe cutis laxa (CL) syndromes are a heterogenous group of multisystemic HCTDs characterized by loose redundant skin folds secondary to severe elastic fiber fragmentation within the skin. Cutis laxa can be either acquired (Table 10.6) or inherited (Tables 10.7 and 10.8); severity and the spectrum of associated manifestations vary significantly by specific underlying cause. \n\n## **Molecular Basis** \n\nHeritable forms of cutis laxa are caused by pathogenic variants in genes related to various steps in the synthesis and organization of elastic fiber-associated ECM proteins, including elastin, LTBPs, and fibulins _(ELN, LTBP4, FBLN4, FBLN5)_ ; histopathologic changes vary based on underlying cause (Fig. 10.12). Heterozygous pathogenic variants in \n\n_ELN_ , which encodes elastin, cause **autosomal dominant cutis laxa (ADCL)** , whereas biallelic pathogenic variants in _LTBP4_ (encoding LTBP-4) and _FBLN4_ and _FBLN5_ (encoding fibulin-4 and fibulin-5) cause different forms of **autosomal recessive cutis laxa (ARCL)** ; phenotypic findings and associated comorbidities vary by underlying cause (Fig. 10.13). Pathogenic variants in additional genes lead to defects in cellular trafficking (e.g., _ATP6V0A2, ATP6V1E1, ATP6V1A, SCYL1BP1,_ and _RIN2_ ) and metabolism (e.g., _ALDH18A1, PYCR1, SLC2A10_ ), suggesting that disturbed intracellular transport, lysosomal dysfunction, mitochondrial dysfunction, and proline synthesis all play roles in pathogenesis of cutis laxa. \n\n**Occipital horn syndrome** , previously referred to as **X-linked cutis laxa** , is caused by pathogenic variants in _ATP7A_ , the same gene that causes **Menkes disease** and **_ATP7A_ -related distal motor neuropathy** (also known as **distal spinal muscular atrophy-3** ). The three conditions are considered _allelic_ disorders on a _phenotypic spectrum_ , with the constellation and severity of findings being related to the level of residual copper transporter function and its effect on downstream copper-dependent enzymes such as lysyl oxidase, dopamine β-hydroxylase, tyrosinase, and cytochrome C oxidase. Features of occipital horn syndrome include generalized cutis laxa, pathognomonic bony extoses on the occiput (Fig. 10.14), bladder diverticula, and mild to moderate intellectual disability. \n\n## **Clinical Manifestations** \n\nCutis laxa syndromes are characterized by generalized _cutis laxa_ , skin that is loose (lax), wrinkled, sagging, redundant,\n\n<!-- p.213 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n##### **10.5** \n\n### **The Differential Diagnosis of Marfan Syndrome** \n\n|Differential Diagnosis (Genes)|Cardiac Features|Vascular Features|Systemic Features|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Aortic Aneurysm Syndromes**||||\n|Loeys-Dietz syndromes<br>(types I-V)<br>_TGFBR1_,_TGFBR2_,_SMAD3_,<br>_TGFB2_,_TGFB3_|• Patent ductus<br>arteriosus<br>• Atrial septal defect<br>• Bicuspid aortic valve|• Aortic root aneurysm<br>• Arterial tortuosity<br>• Widespread aneurysms<br>• Vascular dissection at<br>relatively young ages and<br>small aortic dimensions|• Hypertelorism<br>• Cleft palate<br>• Broad or bifid uvula<br>• Craniosynostosis<br>• Midface hypoplasia<br>• Blue sclerae<br>• Arachnodactyly<br>• Pectus deformity<br>• Scoliosis<br>• Joint hypermobility<br>• Pes planus<br>• Rarely easy bruising<br>• Dystrophic scars<br>• Translucent skin<br>• Rarely developmental delay|\n|Familial thoracic aortic aneurysm<br>_TGFBR2,_ _ACTA2_, others|• Generally none<br>• Rare forms with patent<br>ductus arteriosus|• Aortic root aneurysm<br>• Ascending aortic<br>aneurysm|• Generally none<br>• Rarely livedo reticularis and iris<br>flocculi|\n|Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome<br>_SKI,_unknown others|None|• Aortic root aneurysm|• Hypertelorism<br>• Craniosynostosis<br>• Arched palate<br>• Arachnodactyly<br>• Pectus deformity<br>• Scoliosis<br>• Joint hypermobility<br>• Developmental delay|\n|Bicuspid aortic valve with aortic<br>aneurysm<br>_ACTA2_|Bicuspid aortic valve|• Aortic root aneurysm<br>• Ascending aortic<br>aneurysm||\n|Vascular Ehlers-Danlos<br>syndrome<br>_COL3A1_|Mitral valve prolapse|• Aneurysm and rupture of<br>any medium to large<br>muscular artery<br>• No predisposition for<br>aortic root enlargement|• Joint hypermobility<br>• Atrophic scars<br>• Translucent skin<br>• Easy bruising<br>• Hernias<br>• Rupture of hollow organs|\n|**Ectopia Lentis Syndromes**|||i|\n|Familial ectopia lentis<br>_FBN1,_ _LTBP2_,_ADAMTSL4_|None|None|Nonspecific skeletal features|\n|Homocystinuria<br>_CBS_|Mitral valve prolapse|Intravascular thrombosis|• Tall stature<br>• Ectopia lentis<br>• Long-bone overgrowth<br>• Developmental delay|\n|**Syndromes With Systemic M**|**anifestations of MFS**|||\n|MASS (mitral valve, aorta,<br>skeleton, skin) phenotype<br>_FBN1_|Mitral valve prolapse|Borderline or nonprogressive|• Nonspecific skin and skeletal<br>findings<br>• Myopia|\n|From Doyle JJ, Dietz HC. Marfan syndrom|e. In:_Nelson Textbook of Pediatric_|_s_. 22nd ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025|(Table 743.4, p. 4367).|\n\n\nand inelastic. When stretched, inelastic skin returns to place _very slowly_ . This inelasticity differs from the skin hyperextensibility observed in EDS, where the skin easily _snaps back_ after it is released. The skin in the lower facial, axillary, and inguinal regions is most commonly affected. \n\nAbnormal skin may give affected individuals a prematurely aged appearance. \n\nAssociated findings vary by underlying cause (Table 10.7). Individuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL are at risk of **aortic root dilatation** and **emphysema** (Fig. 10.15). _FBLN4_ -related\n\n<!-- p.214 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n### **Acquired Cutis Laxa: Associated Conditions** \n\n##### **10.6** \n\n#### **Skin Disorders** \n\n|• Urticaria and/or angioedema|\n|---|\n|• Drug eruptions (e.g., due to penicillin or an SSRI)|\n|• Sweet syndrome-like eruptions (Marshall syndrome*)|\n|• Urticarial vasculitis|\n|• Arthropod bite reactions|\n|• Erythema multiforme|\n|• Dermatitis herpetiformis|\n|• Lupus panniculitis|\n|• Interstitial granulomatous dermatitis|\n|• Cutaneous mastocytosis|\n|• Cutaneous lymphoma|\n|**Extracutaneous Inflammatory Disorders**|\n|• Rheumatoid arthritis|\n|• Systemic lupus erythematosus<br>• Nephrotic syndrome<br>• Celiac disease<br>• Sarcoidosis|\n|**Hematologic Disorders**|\n|• Monoclonal gammopathy (especially acral CL;<br>underlying plasma cell dyscrasia)<sup>†</sup>|\n|• Congenital hemolytic anemia|\n|**Infectious Diseases**<br>|\n|• Febrile viral syndrome|\n|•_Borrelia burgdorferi_infection<br>|\n|• Syphilis|\n|• Leprosy|\n|**Drug Intake(Also See Drug Eruptions Above)**|\n|• Penicillamine (embryopathy)<br>• Isoniazid|\n|Generalized or localized CL (including Marshall syndrome) can occur in<br>the setting of α1-antitrypsin deficiency.<br>_SSRI,_Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.<br>*Occurs primarily in infants and young children and is referred to as type<br>II acquired CL, with other forms of acquired CL classified as type I.<br>†Commonly associated with systemic amyloidosis in setting of multiple<br>myeloma.|\n|From Burrows NP, Ringpfeil F, Uillo J. Heritable disorders of connective<br>tissue. In: Bolognia JL, Schaffer JV, Cerroni L, eds._Dermatology_. 5th ed.,<br>Vol. 2. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025 (Table 97.8, p. 1727).|\n\n\nARCL is characterized by widespread arterial tortuosity, aneurysms, and stenoses. _FBLN5_ and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL overlap extensively and present with life-threatening pulmonary emphysema, arterial stenoses, and diverticula of the gastrointestinal and urinary tracts, and distinguishing between the two on clinical grounds alone is challenging. The overall prognosis of _FBLN5_ - and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL is poor, with very few individuals surviving beyond childhood. Premature death results mostly from cardiopulmonary failure due to complications of pulmonary emphysema and, in the case of _FBLN5_ -related ARCL, due to diverticula in hollow viscera. \n\n## **_Skin_** \n\nIndividuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL have generalized but variable skin involvement that ranges from mildly \n\nhyperextensible skin to loose, redundant skin folds that are most pronounced in the lower facial, axillary, and inguinal regions. Individuals with _FBLN4_ -related ARCL have mild skin findings, typically consisting of hyperextensible or velvety skin, or scattered redundant skin folds in the axillae and groin. Rarely, inguinal or diaphragmatic hernias are observed. In contrast, individuals with _FBLN5_ and _LTBP4_ - related ARCL have severe generalized cutis laxa with loose, redundant skin folds over the whole body, particularly in the neck, axillae, and groin (Fig. 10.16). The generalized skin sagging results in a drooped or prematurely aged facial appearance with sagging cheeks. Inguinal hernias are frequent in _FBLN5_ -related ARCL. \n\n## **_Musculoskeletal_** \n\nIndividuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL, _FBLN4_ -related ARCL, and to a lesser extent _LTBP4_ -related ARCL display joint hypermobility. _FBLN4_ -related ARCL is also associated with arachnodactyly, pectus deformities, and osteoporosis with easy fracturing. \n\n## **_Cardiovascular_** \n\nSome individuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL develop aortic root dilatation with a risk for dissection, while some have bicuspid aortic valves with dilatation more distally in the ascending aorta. _FBLN4_ -related ARCL is associated with severe cardiovascular abnormalities including aortic dilatation, aortic and arterial tortuosity, focal aortic narrowing at the isthmus, and dilatation or stenosis of the pulmonary arteries, which leads to a higher risk for life-threatening complications including stroke, dissection, and cardiopulmonary failure. \n\n**Supravalvular aortic stenosis** and **peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis** are the most common cardiovascular abnormalities in _FBLN5_ -related ARCL. Peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis often leads to right ventricular dilatation and progressive heart failure. Tricuspid valve dysplasia, including stenosis and regurgitation, may be present. \n\nPeripheral pulmonary artery stenosis—but not supravalvular aortic stenosis—is also a hallmark of _LTBP4_ -related ARCL and is often complicated by pulmonary hypertension, a feature specific for _LTBP4_ -related ARCL. Septal defects and valvular dysfunction are less common. \n\n## **_Pulmonary_** \n\nApproximately 35% of individuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL develop obstructive lung disease and emphysema, predisposing to airway infections and bronchiectasis. Severe emphysema requiring lung transplantation has been observed in affected individuals who are heavy smokers. _FBLN4_ -related ARCL is usually not associated with pulmonary emphysema, but aneurysmatic compression of the trachea and bronchi may cause respiratory complications such as atelectasis and respiratory distress. In contrast, _FBLN5_ and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL are associated with severe pulmonary emphysema, usually becoming obvious in the first months of life or in early childhood. Recurrent lower respiratory infections resulting in pneumonia are reported in most patients.\n\n<!-- p.215 -->\n\n### **TABLE Heritable Forms of Cutis Laxa and Related Conditions 10.7** \n\n|Disorder|Inheri-<br>tance|Gene|Protein|Distribution of Cutis<br>Laxa|Additional Features|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Heritable Forms of**|**Cutis Laxa**|||||\n|ADCL|AD*|_ELN_<br>_FBLN5_(single<br>patient<br>reported)|Elastin<br>Fibulin 5|Generalized|• Cardiac valve abnormalities, aortic dilatation (variable)<br>• Emphysema (uncommon)<br>• Hernias<sup>†</sup>|\n|ARCL type 1 A|AR|_FBLN5_|Fibulin 5|Generalized; large ears<br>with prominent lobes|• Pulmonary emphysema<br>• Stenoses of the supravalvular aorta and pulmonary arteries<br>• Gastrointestinal and vesicourinary diverticula, hernias<sup>†</sup><br>• Micrognathia|\n|ARCL type 1B|AR|_FBLN4/EFEMP2_|Fibulin 4|Generalized but less<br>pronounced; may favor<br>skin folds|<br>• Arterial or aortic tortuosity/aneurysms; aortic isthmus<br>stenosis<br>• Joint hypermobility, arachnodactyly, fractures, high arched<br>palate, micrognathia|\n|ARCL type 1 C<br>(Urban–Rifkin–Davis<br>syndrome)|AR|_LTBP4_|Latent TGF-βbinding<br>protein-4|Generalized|• Emphysema (severe, often fatal)<br>• Pulmonary arterial stenosis, valvular defects<br>• GI and GU malformations (diverticula, rupture risk)<br>• Micrognathia, large fontanelles<br>• Joint hypermobility, hypotonia<br>• Bitemporal hypotrichosis|\n|ARCL type 2 A (Debré<br>type; allelic to<br>wrinkly skin<br>syndrome)|AR|_ATP6V0A2_|Vacuolar H<sup>+</sup>-ATPase V0,<br>subunit A2|Generalized; may favor<br>abdomen and dorsal<br>hands; improves over<br>time|• Translucent skin with prominent veins<br>• High myopia (2 A); corneal opacities and cataracts (3 A/B,<br>PADCL)<br>• Facial dysmorphism<sup>‡</sup>; triangular face with more progeroid<br>|\n|ARCL types 2B and<br>3B (de Barsy<br>syndrome B)|AR|_PYCR1_|Pyrroline-5-carboxylate<br>reductase 1|Generalized; may favor<br>dorsal hands/feet or<br>improve over time;<br>occasionally fat pads<br>(e.g., on buttocks; 3 A)|appearance (2B, 3 A/B); large fontanelles<br>• Microcephaly, partial pachygyria (2 A), absent corpus<br>callosum (2B)<br>• Intellectual disability; seizures, neurologic degeneration<br>(2 A); dystonia/athetosis (3 A/B)<br>• Congenital hip dysplasia, joint hypermobility, hypotonia,<br>scoliosis, short stature; osteopenia (2B, 3 A/B)<br>• Cranial arterial tortuosity (3 A, PADCL)<br>• O- and N-glycosylation defect (2 A<sup>§</sup>)|\n|ARCL type 3 A (de<br>Barsy syndrome A)<br>and Progeroid<br>ADCL (PADCL)|AR (3 A)<br>AD<br>(PADCL|_P5CS/_<br>_ALDH18A1_|Pyrroline-5-carboxylate<br>synthetase|||\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.216 -->\n\n### **TABLE Heritable Forms of Cutis Laxa and Related Conditions—cont’d 10.7** \n\n|Disorder|Inheri-<br>tance|Gene|Protein|Distribution of Cutis<br>Laxa|Additional Features|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|ARCL types 2 C and<br>2D|AR|_ATP6V1E1_(2 C)<br>_ATP6V1A_(2D)|Vacuolar H<sup>+</sup>-ATPase V<sup>+</sup>,<br>subunits E1 and A|Generalized with sparse<br>or subcutaneous fat;<br>large skin folds on<br>extremities, fat pads<br>on buttocks; may<br>improve over time (2D)|• Pneumothorax<br>• Cardiac abnormalities, aortic dilation<br>• “Mask-like” triangular face, hypotonia; variable hip dysplasia,<br>marfanoid habitus<br>• Neurodevelopmental abnormalities (2D)|\n|Gerodermia<br>osteodysplastica<br>(geroderma<br>osteodysplasticum)|AR|_GORAB_|Golgin, RAB6-interacting|Primarily on dorsal<br>hands/feet|• Malar hypoplasia, prognathism, oblique furrow lateral to<br>eyebrows<br>• Joint hypermobility<br>• Osteoporosis, fractures, dwarfism|\n|Occipital horn<br>syndrome|X-R|_ATP7A_|ATPase, Cu<sup>2+</sup>-<br>transporting,<br>α-polypeptide|Generalized, most<br>pronounced on face<br>and neck|• Easy bruising, coarse hair (variable)<br>• Arterial tortuosity<br>• Chronic diarrhea; bladder diverticula<br>• Long face, high forehead, hooked nose<br>• Developmental delay<br>• Wedge-shaped occipital calcifications<br>• Joint hypermobility|\n|Macrocephaly,<br>alopecia, CL, and<br>scoliosis (MACS)|AR|_RIN2_|RAS and RAB interactor 2|Generalized, most<br>pronounced on face|• Sparse scalp hair<br>• Gingival hypertrophy, facial dysmorphism<br>• Macrocephaly<br>• Scoliosis, joint hypermobility|\n|**Other Heritable Co**|**nditions Th**|**at Feature Lax and**|**/or Redundant Skin**|||\n|Arterial tortuosity<br>syndrome|AR|_SLC2A10_<br>_(GLUT10)_|Solute carrier family 2,<br>member 10 (a glucose<br>transporter)|Generalized; soft,<br>hyperextensible skin<br>has also been<br>described|• Telangiectasias on cheeks (variable)<br>• Arterial tortuosity<br>• Long face, micrognathia, high-arched palate<br>• Joint hypermobility and contractures|\n|Larsen-like syndrome|AR|_B3GAT3_|Glucuronosyltransferase I|Wrinkled acral skin|• Joint dislocations, craniofacial dysmorphism, short stature<br>• Congenital heart defects|\n|Costello syndrome|AR|_HRAS_|HRAS|Variable, especially on<br>hands and feet|• Deep palmoplantar creases, acanthosis nigricans, periorificial<br>papillomas<br>• Curly or sparse hair, coarse facies<br>• Cardiac malformations<br>• Intellectual disability, short stature<br>• Risk of malignancies, e.g., rhabdomyosarcoma|\n|Donohue syndrome<br>(“leprechaunism”)|AR|_INSR_|Insulin receptor|Variable|• Generalized lipodystrophy, hypertrichosis, acanthosis<br>nigricans<br>• Insulin resistance<br>• Distinctive “elfin” facies<br>• Severe growth delay|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.217 -->\n\n### **TABLE Heritable Forms of Cutis Laxa and Related Conditions—cont’d 10.7** \n\n|Disorder|Inheri-<br>tance|Gene|Protein|Distribution of Cutis<br>Laxa|Additional Features|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Finnish-type<br>amyloidosis|AD|_GSN_|Gelsolin|Generalized|• Corneal dystrophy<br>• Polyneuropathy|\n|Cranioectodermal<br>dysplasia<br>|AR|_IFT122_<br>_IFT43_|Intraflagellar transport<br>proteins 122 and 43|Generalized; redundant<br>skin folds at neck,<br>|• Sparse, fine hair; short, broad nails<br>• Narrow thorax, short proximal limbs, joint hypermobility,<br>|\n|(Sensenbrenner<br>syndrome)||_WDR35_<br>WDR19|WD repeat domain 35<br>and 19|ankles, and wrists|brachydactyly, dolichocephaly, dysmorphic facies, dental<br>anomalies<br>• Nephronophthisis, hepatic fibrosis, retinal dystrophy|\n|Circumferential skin<br>creases, Kunze<br>type|AD, AR<br>AD|_MAPRE2_<br>_TUBB_|Microtubule-associated<br>protein RP/EB family<br>member 2<br>Tubulin-βclass I|Multiple circumferential<br>creases/folds of skin<br>on the extremities><br>neck>trunk (excess<br>rather than lax skin);<br>may resolve over time|• Cleft palate, short stature, dysmorphic facies (e.g., epicanthal<br>folds, small eyes), overfolded helices<br>• Intellectual disability|\n|Nonsyndromic<br>“Michelin tire baby”|AD|?|?||• Affected skin may have excess fat or smooth muscle, and<br>the latter is often associated with hypertrichosis; elastic fibers<br>are usually normal (fragmentation noted in only one report)|\n\n\nCL can also occur in Williams–Beuren syndrome, which results from deletion of multiple contiguous genes that include _ELN_ . In addition to ARCL types 2A and 2 C, CL can occur in other congenital disorders of glycosylation, e.g., due to variants in a component of oligomeric Golgi complex 7 ( _COG7_ ; multiple-system malformations) or steroid 5-α-reductase 3 ( _SRD5A3;_ also ichthyosis, ocular colobomas, and midline brain malformations). Transaldolase deficiency can present with neonatal CL (generalized or of the forehead) that improves over time as well as hypertrichosis, hepatosplenomegaly, hemolytic anemia, and GU malformations. Features of cutis laxa have also been reported in PURA syndrome. “Neonatal CL with a marfanoid phenotype” was described in an infant who lacked immunohistochemical evidence of laminin-β1 in the skin. \n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _ALDH18A1_ , aldehyde dehydrogenase 18 family, member A1; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _EFEMP2_ , EGF-containing fibulin-like extracellular matrix protein 2; _GI,_ gastrointestinal; _GU,_ genitourinary; _TGF,_ transforming growth factor; _X-R,_ X-linked recessive. \n\n*An AR form of cutis laxa due to a homozygous elastin variant has been reported in two related families. \n\n†E.g., inguinal, umbilical, diaphragmatic. \n\n‡Features may include a broad forehead and midfacial hypoplasia as well as findings related to the lax skin (e.g., down-slanting palpebral fissures, long philtrum). \n\n§Can be assessed via analysis of the glycosylation of serum sialotransferrin ± apolipoprotein C-III via isoelectric focusing. \n\nFrom Burrows NP, Ringpfeil F, Uillo J. Heritable disorders of connective tissue. In: Bolognia JL, Schaffer JV, Cerroni L, eds. _Dermatology_ . 5th ed., Vol. 2. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025 (Table 97.7, p. 1725–1726).\n\n<!-- p.218 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.8** Additional Syndromes Featuring La x or Wrinkled Skin\n\n|Syndrome|Gene|Cutaneous Findings|Other Predominant Findings|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Geroderma<br>osteodysplasticum|_SCYL1BP1_|Lax, nonhyperelastic skin most<br>prominent over extremities;<br>premature aging|Osteoporosis, joint laxity, AR inheritance|\n|Cantu syndrome|_ABCC9_<br>KCNJ8<br>Others|Hypertrichosis, wrinkled palms<br>and soles|Delayed psychomotor development, short<br>stature, large head, peculiar facies,<br>cardiac abnormalities, mild ID,<br>osteochondrodysplasia, AD inheritance|\n|SCARF syndrome|Unknown|CL, webbed neck|Joint hyperextensibility, umbilical and<br>inguinal herniae, skeletal abnormalities,<br>craniosynostosis, ambiguous genitalia,<br>cognitive impairment, facial abnormalities|\n|Williams syndrome<br>(Williams-Beuren<br>syndrome)|Deletion of multiple<br>genes in 7q11.23<br>including_ELN_|Premature wrinkling and aging,<br>facial anomalies|AD inheritance, supravalvular aortic<br>stenosis, multiple peripheral pulmonary<br>artery stenosis, mental deficiency, short<br>stature, dental malformations, infantile<br>hypercalcemia|\n|Patterson syndrome<br>(pseudo-leprechaunism)|Unknown|Cutis gyrata of hands and feet,<br>bronze hyperpigmentation|Hyperadrenocorticism, diabetes mellitus,<br>bladder diverticula, severe development<br>delay, major bony deformities|\n|Hutchinson-Gilford<br>syndrome|_LMNA_<br>_Lamin A_|Wrinkled, aged-looking skin; loss<br>of subcutaneous fat; alopecia|<br>Progeria, short stature, micrognathia,<br>craniofacial disproportion, joint stiffness|\n|Kabuki syndrome|_KMT2D_<br>_KDM6A_|CL (rare), abnormal facies,<br>dermatoglyphic abnormalities|ID, postnatal growth deficiency, skeletal<br>anomalies, recurrent otitis media in<br>infancy|\n|Barber-Say syndrome|_TWIST2_|Generalized hypertrichosis;<br>ectropion of eyelids;<br>redundant, lax skin|Ambiguous genitalia, macrostomia|\n|Congenital hemolytic<br>anemia with emphysema<br>and cutis laxa|<br>Unknown|CL|Hemolytic anemia, emphysema,<br>hemorrhagic necrosis of adrenals|\n|Ablepharon macrostomia<br>syndrome|_TWIST2_|Redundant, lax skin; absence<br>or hypoplasia of eyelids|Ambiguous genitalia, abnormal ears,<br>rudimentary nipples, macrostomia|\n|Lenz-Majewski<br>hyperostotic dwarfism|_PTDSS1_|Thin, wrinkled, and atrophic<br>skin; prominent cutaneous<br>veins|Delayed closure of fontanelle, ID,<br>progressive skeletal sclerosis with severe<br>growth retardation|\n|GAPO syndrome|_ANTXR1_|Redundant facial skin|Growth restriction, alopecia,<br>pseudoanodontia, optic atrophy, facial<br>dysmorphism, short stature, glaucoma|\n\n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _CL,_ cutis laxa; _ID,_ intellectual disability; _SCARF,_ skeletal abnormalities, cutis laxa, craniostenosis, ambiguous genitalia, psychomotor retardation, facial dysmorphism. Modified from Berk DR, Bentley DD, Bayliss SJ, et al. Cutis laxa: a review. _J Am Acad Dermatol_ . 2012;66(5):842.e1-e17 (Table IV, pp. 842.e12-e13). \n\n## **_Orofacial Features_** \n\nIndividuals with _ELN_ -related ADCL may have a typical facial appearance with a high forehead, sagging cheeks, prominent nasolabial folds, large ears, a long philtrum, and a convex nasal ridge; a hoarse voice is also common. Individuals with _FBLN4_ -related ARCL frequently have hypertelorism, a long philtrum with a thin upper vermilion, a highly arched palate, and retrognathia. _FBLN5_ and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL are characterized by a drooped or prematurely aged facial appearance with sagging cheeks and micrognathia. \n\n## **_Other Features_** \n\n_FBLN5_ and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL is associated with bladder diverticula, vesicourethral reflux (VUR), and recurrent upper and lower urinary tract infections. Gastrointestinal abnormalities are a discernable feature of _LTBP4_ -related ARCL; newborns are at risk for early-onset pyloric stenosis. Diverticula, elongation, and tortuosity of the gastrointestinal tract predispose to intestinal wall fragility and rupture. Rectal prolapse may occur and cause obstipation. Diaphragmatic involvement is present in half of affected individuals\n\n<!-- p.219 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.9** The Sti ckler Syndromes and Al lied Collagenopathies\n\n|Syndrome||Gene|Distinguishing Features|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Stickler<br>Syndrome|Type 1|COL2A1|Type 1. Membranous congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment, congenital<br>megalophthalmos, deafness, arthropathy, cleft palate<br>High risk of blindness|\n||Ocular only|COL2A1|Type 1. Membranous congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment congenital<br>megalophthalmos. No systemic features.<br>High risk of blindness|\n||Type 2|COL11A1|Beaded type 2 congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment, congenital<br>megalophthalmos, deafness, arthropathy, cleft palate|\n||Type 2<br>Recessive|COL11A1|Autosomal recessive, Beaded congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment,<br>congenital megalophthalmos, cleft palate, profound severe congenital deafness|\n||Type 3|COL11A2|Non-ocular Stickler<br>Normal vitreous and ocular phenotype, deafness, arthropathy, cleft palate|\n||Type 4|COL9A1|Recessive inheritance, sensorineural deafness, myopia, vitreoretinopathy, retinal<br>detachment, epiphyseal dysplasia|\n||Type 5|COL9A2|Recessive inheritance, sensorineural deafness, myopia, vitreoretinopathy, retinal<br>detachment, epiphyseal dysplasia|\n||Type 6|COL9A3|Recessive inheritance, sensorineural deafness, myopia, vitreoretinopathy, retinal<br>detachment, epiphyseal dysplasia|\n||Type 7|BMP4|Hypoplastic vitreous, retinal detachment deafness, arthropathy, palate abnormality,<br>renal dysplasia|\n||Type 8|LOXL3|Recessive inheritance<br>Congenital myopia, hypoplastic vitreous, palate abnormality<br>Arthropathy<br>Normal facies<br>Normal hearing|\n|Kniest<br>Dysplasia||COL2A1|(usually) Type 1 Membranous congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment,<br>congenital megalophthalmos, severe arthropathy, short stature, phalangeal<br>dysplasia|\n|SEDC||COL2A1|(usually) Type 1 Membranous congenital vitreous anomaly, retinal detachment,<br>congenital megalophthalmos, severe short stature, rhizomelic limb shortening,<br>barrel chest|\n|Marfan<br>Syndrome||FBN1|Developmental myopia, retinal detachment, iris abnormality, cornea plana,<br>arachnodactyly and joint laxity, pneumothorax, hernia, aortic root, and<br>cardiovascular abnormality|\n|Wagner<br>Syndrome||VCAN|Early cataract, pseudoexotropia, erosive vitreoretinopathy|\n|Czech<br>Syndrome||COL2A1|Hypoplastic vitreous, retinal detachment, cleft palate, normal stature,<br>spondyloarthropathy, short postaxial toes|\n\n\n_SEDC,_ Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita. Modified from Snead M, Martin H, Bale P, et al. Therapeutic and diagnostic advances in Stickler syndrome. _Ther Adv Rare Dis._ 2020;1:1-13 (Table 1). \n\nand includes congenital, sliding, or hiatal hernia, as well as diaphragmatic eventration, frequently aggravating respiratory problems. Gastroesophageal reflux is common. \n\n_ELN_ -related ADCL is approximately 50, while _FBLN4_ , _FBLN5_ , and _LTBP4_ -related ARCL are even rarer. \n\n## **Differential Diagnosis** \n\n## **Epidemiology** \n\nReliable data on the incidence of cutis laxa are not available. The number of affected individuals or families with \n\nCutis laxa should be distinguished from EDS and pseudoxanthoma elasticum. EDS is distinguished by the presence of hyperelastic skin that readily snaps back into place after\n\n<!-- p.220 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0221_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.12** Histochemical and electron microscopic analysis of elastic fibers in cutis laxa (CL). Hart elastin (e) stain of skin sections from healthy donor (A), individual with autosomal dominant CL-I (ADCL-I) and elastin pathogenic variant (B), individual with autosomal recessive CL-I (ARCL-IA) and fibulin-5 pathogenic variant (C), individual with ARCL-IB and fibulin-4 pathogenic variants (D), and individual with ARCL-IC and LTBP4 variant (E). _Arrowheads_ indicate elaunin fibers in papillary dermis, and arrows show elastic fibers in reticular dermis. Note paucity of elaunin fibers in CL skin (B to E) and either underdeveloped (B and D) or globular (C and E) elastin deposits in reticular dermis. Electron microscopic analysis of elastic fibers from healthy donor (F), individual with ADCL and _elastin_ pathogenic variant (G), patient with ARCL-IA and fibulin-5 variant (H), and patient with ARCLIC and LTBP4 variant (I). Note the close association of microfibrils (mf) with elastin in the control and elastin deposits devoid of microfibrils in all 3 CL samples. col, Collagen fibrils. Magnification bars: 50 μm (A to D), 20 μm (E), and 200 nm (F to I). (From Berk DR, Bentley DD, Bayliss SJ, Lind A, Urban Z. Cutis laxa: a review. _J Am Acad Dermatol_ . 2012;66(5):842.e1-842.e17.) \n\nbeing stretched, while cutix laxa is characterized by loose skin with delayed elastic recoil. Lax or wrinkled skin is a feature in a number of other syndromic conditions (Table 10.8). \n\n# **The Diagnosis of Hereditary Connective Tissue Disorders** \n\nThe accurate diagnosis of a specific HCTD is often challenged by the multisystemic, complex, and phenotypically variable nature of most HCTDs, limited clinician knowledge on typical and atypical presentations, and uncertainty among many providers on appropriate diagnostic approaches. Diagnosis is often delayed or mistaken. Diagnostic evaluation should focus not only on defining the phenotypic features of the patient, but also on identifying phenotypic traits in affected or potentially affected family members, which can aid in refining the clinical classification into a more specific disorder or class of \n\ndisorders. Evaluation should focus first on the most common disorders, recognizing that the signs and symptoms may vary among different individuals and family members with the same diagnosis. \n\n## **Clinical Assessment** \n\nIn general, _autosomal recessive_ forms of EDS are associated with congenital or early-onset problems, including hip or other joint dislocations, talipes equinovarus, congenital umbilical hernia, and severe muscle hypotonia leading to floppy infant syndrome, often leading to earlier recognition and diagnosis. For _autosomal dominant_ forms such as classical and vascular EDS, diagnosis is often missed in childhood due to the presence of only subtle signs of connective tissue fragility that are common in the general population (e.g., easy bruising and joint hypermobility). The 2017 International EDS Consortium classification guides the diagnosis of EDS, defining\n\n<!-- p.221 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0222_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.13** Manifestations of autosomal recessive cutis laxa type I. (A) Redundant skin and large ears in an individual with fibulin-5 pathogenic variant. (B) Sagging cheeks and periorbital area, reverse-V eyebrows, long philtrum, and large ears in an individual with fibulin-4 pathogenic variant. (C) Redundant skin and joint laxity on the legs of an individual with ARCL-I. Computed tomography images of tortuous aorta _(arrows)_ and diaphragmatic hernia _(arrowhead)_ in an individual with fibulin-4 variant (D) and aortic aneurysm (arrow) in an individual with fibulin-4 variant (E). (From DR Berk, DD Bentley, SJ Bayliss, A Lind, Z Urban, Cutis laxa: a review. _J Am Acad Dermatol_ . 2012;66[5]:842.e1-842.e17.) \n\nmajor and minor clinical criteria for each of 13 clinically defined EDS types to aid in phenotypic diagnosis and inform genetic testing choices (Table 10.4). A major criterion is expected to have high diagnostic specificity as it is present in most individuals with that type of EDS and is absent or rare in the general population. By contrast, a minor criterion conveys less diagnostic specificity, but its presence supports the diagnosis, and the combination of several minor criteria is more suggestive of the specific EDS diagnosis. \n\nAll individuals with a suspected diagnosis of Marfan syndrome or one of its related disorders should undergo slit-lamp examination to evaluate for ectopia lentis and an echocardiogram to evaluate for aortic root dilatation. \n\nBecause homocystinuria is also associated with skeletal and eye abnormalities that can mimic Marfan syndrome, plasma amino acid analysis should be obtained to exclude this possibility. **Stickler syndromes** and related collagenopathies should also be considered, especially in the presence of ocular features (Tables 10.1 and 10.9). \n\nThe clinical diagnosis of Marfan syndrome is guided by the international Ghent criteria, which emphasizes two cardinal features, dilation of the ascending aorta with or without dissection, as well as ectopia lentis. Other cardiovascular and ocular manifestations and findings in other organ systems such as the skeleton, dura, skin, and lungs contribute to a systemic score that guides diagnosis when aortic disease\n\n<!-- p.222 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0223_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.14** Cranial CT scan showing occipital horns. (From Fradin M, Lavillaureix A, Jaillard S, et al. ATP7A variant with occipital horns and distal motor neuropathy: a continuum. _Eur J Med Genet._ 2020;63[12]:104087.) \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0223_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.15** Manifestations of autosomal dominant cutis laxa. (A) Photograph of affected individual at age 13 years. (B) Chest radiograph of a 51-year-old individual with severe emphysema shown by bilateral hyperexpanded lung fields. (C) Magnetic resonance imaging of aortic root in an affected individual shows a 5.8 cm aneurysm (white bar). (From Berk DR, Bentley DD, Bayliss SJ, et al. Cutis laxa: a review. _J Am Acad Dermatol_ . 2012;66[5]:842.e1-842.e17.) \n\nis present but ectopia lentis is not (Tables 10.10 and 10.11). The clinical and genetic spectrums of **thoracic aortic aneurysm syndromes** are noted in Table 10.12. \n\nThe diagnosis of a cutis laxa syndrome is often readily suggested by the presence of loose skin with decreased elasticity. Family history can define modes of transmission and guide genetic testing as well as prognostication, as autosomal dominant, and X-linked recessive, and autosomal recessive forms of heritable cutis laxa are associated with different systemic manifestations and degrees of severity. \n\n## **_Molecular Genetic Testing_** \n\n_Molecular genetic testing is the standard for confirming a diagnosis of a HCTD and is crucial for accurate genetic counselling, providing information on the transmission pattern and recurrence risk, and guiding prevention and management strategies._ If a pathogenic variant is known in the family, specific analysis of that variant is the most direct way to confirm or exclude the diagnosis in another family member. In most other situations, diagnostic studies are done using highly parallel sequence analysis (often referred to as\n\n<!-- p.223 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0224_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 10.16** Generalized cutis laxa in a child with _LTBP4_ -related cutis laxa syndrome. \n\nnext generation sequencing or NGS), in which a panel of known genes is sequenced and analyzed simultaneously. Multi gene NGS panels that include the genes of interest for a group of disorders (e.g., EDS gene panel, cutis laxa gene panel, thoracic aortic aneurysm gene panels) or larger gene panels comprising all known HCTD genes are the preferred \n\ndiagnostic approach in case of complex phenotypes because they are more timeand cost-effective than gene-by-gene Sanger sequencing and can identify large and small genomic deletions in the covered regions. Gene panels vary among different laboratories and evolve over time, and the list of genes on a panel and the method of analysis should be confirmed prior to ordering. \n\nThe analysis of two particular EDS genes is complicated. _TNXB_ has a partially duplicated nonfunctional pseudo gene, _TNXA_ , which shares 97% of its sequence identity with the 3′ region of _TNXB_ . Therefore, exons 32–44 of _TNXB_ should be separately analyzed by Sanger sequencing when _TNXB_ is in the list of candidate genes. For _PLOD1_ , an 8.9 kb duplication of exons 10–16 is the most common pathogenic variant, found in up to 30% of individuals with kyphoscoliotic EDS related to _PLOD1_ . This duplication should be detected using a specifically designed PCR probe or via multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification. \n\nWhen sequencing fails to identify a causative genetic defect in any of the known candidate genes, a strategy to detect genomic copy number variants affecting these genes, such as array comparative genomic hybridization or genome sequencing, should be considered. For a considerable percentage of individuals with clearly delineated HCTD phenotypes, still no genetic defect is found, due to either technical limitations (e.g., deep intronic variants, variants in promotor or untranslated regions) or additional genetic heterogeneity with novel, yet to be discovered disease genes. In these instances, where individuals remain undiagnosed despite targeted analysis, broader phenotype-agnostic approaches such as RNA sequencing, exome sequencing, or genome sequencing (WGS) can be considered. \n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n### **10.10 Diagnostic Criteria for Marfan Syndrome** \n\n|**In the Absence of a Family History of MFS, a Diagnosis Can Be Established in Four Distinct Scenarios:**|\n|---|\n|1. Aortic root_z_score≥2 AND ectopia lentis*|\n|2. Aortic root_z_score≥2 AND a bona fide_FBN1_pathogenic variant|\n|3. Aortic root_z_score≥2 AND a_systemic score_ ≥7*(seeTable 10.11)|\n|4. Ectopia lentis AND a bona fide_FBN1_pathogenic variant known to cause aortic disease|\n|**In the Presence of a Family History of MFS, a Diagnosis Can Be Established in the Presence of:**|\n|1. Ectopia lentis|\n|2. A_systemic score_ ≥7*|\n|3. Aortic root_z_score≥2 if older than 20 years or≥3 if younger than 20 years*|\n|**In the Absence of a Family History of MFS, Alternative Diagnoses Include:**|\n|1. Ectopia lentis± _systemic score_AND_FBN1_pathogenic variant not known to associate with aortic aneurysm or no_FBN1_<br>pathogenic variant=Ectopia lentis syndrome|\n|2. Aortic root_z_score<2 AND a systemic score≥5 (with at least one skeletal feature) without ectopia lentis=MASS phenotype|\n\n\n3. Mitral valve prolapse AND aortic root _z_ score <2 AND a _systemic score_ <5 without ectopia lentis = Mitral valve prolapse syndrome \n\n*Denotes caveat that features suggestive of an alternative diagnosis must be excluded and appropriate alternative molecular testing should be performed. Other syndromes include Shprintzen-Goldberg, Loeys-Dietz, or vascular Ehlers Danlos. \n\nFrom Doyle JJ, Dietz III HC: Marfan syndrome in Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 22/e, Vol 2, 2025, Elsevier, Philadelphia (Table 743.1, p. 4365).\n\n<!-- p.224 -->\n\n#### **TABLE** \n\n#### **10.11** \n\n### **Scoring of Systemic Features in Points** \n\n- Wrist AND thumb sign = 3 (wrist OR thumb sign = 1) \n\n- Pectus carinatum deformity = 2 (pectus excavatum or chest asymmetry = 1) \n\n- Hind foot deformity = 2 (plain pes planus = 1) \n\n- Pneumothorax = 2 \n\n- Dural ectasia = 2 \n\n- Protrusio acetabuli = 2 \n\n- Reduced US/LS AND increased arm/height AND no severe scoliosis = 1 \n\n- Scoliosis or thoracolumbar kyphosis = 1 \n\n- Reduced elbow extension = 1 \n\n- Facial features (3/5) = 1 (dolichocephaly, enophthalmos, down-slanting palpebral fissures, midface hypoplasia, retrognathia) \n\n- Skin striae = 1 \n\n- Myopia >3 diopters = 1 \n\n- Mitral valve prolapse (all types) = 1 \n\nMaximum total: 20 points; score ≥7 indicates systemic involvement. _US/LS,_ Upper segment/lower segment ratio. From Doyle JJ, Dietz III HC: Marfan syndrome in Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 22/e, Vol 2, 2025, Elsevier, Philadelphia (Table 743.3, p. 4366). \n\n\n#### **TABLE 10.12** Thoracic Aortic Ane urysm Syndromes and Conditions Du e to a Heritable or Genetic Cause\n\n|Condition|Gene|Clinical Features|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Syndromic HTAD***|||\n|Marfan syndrome|_FBN1_|Aortic root aneurysm, AD, TAA, MVP, long bone<br>overgrowth, scoliosis, pectus deformities, ectopia<br>lentis, myopia, tall stature, PTX, dural ectasia|\n|Loeys-Dietz syndrome|_TGFBR1, TGFBR2,* SMAD3, TGFB2,_<br>_TGFB3, SMAD2_|TAA, branch vessel aneurysms, AD, arterial tortuosity,<br>MVP, craniosynostosis, hypertelorism, bluish sclera,<br>bifid/broad uvula, translucent skin, visible veins,<br>club feet, dural ectasia,* premature osteoarthritis|\n|Vascular Ehlers-Danlos<br>syndrome|_COL3A1_|TAA, AAA, arterial rupture, AD, MVP, bowel and<br>uterine rupture, PTX, translucent skin, atrophic<br>scars, small joint hypermobility, easy bruising,<br>carotid-cavernous fistula|\n|Arterial tortuosity syndrome|_SLC2A10_|Tortuous large-and medium-sized arteries, aortic<br>dilatation, craniofacial, skin and skeletal features|\n|Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome|_SKI_|Craniosynostosis, skeletal features, aortic dilatation|\n|Congenital contractural<br>arachnodactyly (Beals<br>syndrome)|_FBN2_|MVP, arachnodactyly, Marfanoid habitus, digital<br>contractures, mild aortic dilatation|\n|Cutis laxa|_EFEMP2_(Fibulin-4)|TAA, arterial tortuosity, arterial stenosis, hypertelorism,<br>arachnodactyly|\n|EDS with periventricular<br>nodular heterotopia (PVNH)|_FLNA (filamin A)_|X-linked, PVNH, TAA, BAV, MV disease, PDA, VSD,<br>seizures, joint hypermobility|\n|Meester-Loeys syndrome|_BGN_|X-linked, TAA, AD, skeletal abnormalities|\n|LOX-related TAA|LOX (lysyl oxidase)|TAA, BAV, AD, Marfanoid habitus in some|\n|**Nonsyndromic HTAD(Fam**|**ilial TAA)**||\n|FTAA|_ACTA2_(α-smooth muscle actin)|TAA, AD, BAV, Moya-Moya, premature CAD and<br>CVD, livedo reticularis, iris flocculi|\n|FTAA|_MYH11_(Myosin heavy chain-11)|TAA, AD, PDA|\n|FTAA|_MYLK_(Myosin light chain kinase)|AD at relatively small aortic size|\n|FTAA|_PRKG1_(Protein kinase cGMP-dependent)|Aortic root aneurysm and AD|\n|FTAA|_MAT2A_(MAT IIα)|TAA, AD, BAV|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.225 -->\n\n### **TABLE 10.12 Thoracic Aortic Aneurysm Syndromes and Conditions Due to a Heritable or Genetic Cause—cont’d** \n\n|Condition|Gene|Clinical Features|\n|---|---|---|\n|FTAA|_MFAP5_(microfibrillar-associated protein 5)|TAA, AD, skeletal features may be present|\n|FTAA|_FOXE3_(forkhead transcription factor)|TAA, AD|\n|**Bicuspid Aortic Valve/Ass**|**ociated Ascending Aortic Aneurysm**||\n|Familial BAV/AS and TAA|_NOTCH1_(NOTCH1)|Aortic stenosis, TAA|\n|_TGFBR1, TGFBR2, TGFB2,_<br>_TGFB3, ACTA2, MAT2A,_<br>_GATA5, SMAD6, LOX,_<br>_ROBO4, TBX20_|BAV with TAA|Syndromic and nonsyndromic FTAA with an increased<br>frequency of BAV|\n|Turner syndrome|XO, Xp|BAV, COA, TAA, AD, short stature, lymphedema,<br>webbed neck, premature ovarian failure, affects<br>1 in 2,500 live-born girls|\n\n\n_*_ Some individuals with pathogenic variants in a gene which can lead to syndromic HTAD have very few or no syndromic features and variants in some genes causing syndromic HTAD may also lead to nonsyndromic HTAD. _AAA,_ Abdominal aortic aneurysm; _AAT,_ aortic aneurysm syndrome; _AD,_ aortic dissection; _BAV,_ bicuspid aortic valve; _CAD,_ coronary artery disease; _COA,_ coarctation of the aorta; _CVD,_ cerebrovascular disease; _EDS,_ Ehlers-Danlos syndrome; _FTAA,_ familial thoracic aortic aneurysm (and dissection) syndrome; _HTAD,_ heritable TAA; _LDS,_ Loeys-Dietz syndrome; _MFS,_ Marfan syndrome; _MV,_ mitral valve; _MVP,_ mitral valve prolapse; _PDA,_ patent ductus arteriosus; _PTX,_ pneumothorax; _TAA,_ thoracic aortic aneurysm; _TGF,_ transforming growth factor; _VSD,_ ventricular septal defect. From Braverman AC, Schermerhorn M. Diseases of the aorta. In: Libby P, Bonow R, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Braunwald’s Heart Disease—A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine_ . 12th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022 (Table 42.2, p. 811). \n\nVariants of uncertain significance (VUSs) are common when obtaining molecular genetic studies for HCTDs. When identified in an individual with a suspected HCTD, no treatment decisions or genetic counseling should be offered until the variant is analyzed further or reclassified. Family segregation analysis can provide valuable information on whether a variant is benign or pathogenic; for some genes, additional functional studies can also help reclassify a VUS. In other instances, biochemical assays may be required. \n\n## **_Biochemical Assays_** \n\nFor EDS several biochemical assays can analyze the pathogenicity of a VUS. Sodium dodecyl sulfate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) of radioactively or fluorescently labeled collagens and procollagens can show quantitative and qualitative alterations in type I and III procollagen, which can be helpful for VUSs in _COL1A1, COL1A2, COL3A1, PLOD1_ , and _ADAMTS2_ . SDS-PAGE collagen and procollagen analysis of type V collagen is not sensitive and thus not helpful in functional analysis of _COL5A1_ or _COL5A2_ variants. \n\nQuantification of lysylpyridinoline (LP) and hydroxylysylpyridinoline (HP) crosslinks in urine using high-performance liquid chromatography is a reliable method of detecting LH1 defects, particularly in kyphoscoliotic EDS due to pathogenic variants in _PLOD1_ , or kEDS PLOD1_ . The typical LP/HP ratio in unaffected individuals is 0.2, whereas individuals with kEDS PLOD1_ have increased ratios ranging from 2 to 9. A mildly increased LP/HP ratio of around 1 is seen in individuals with spondylodysplastic EDS due pathogenic variants in _SLC39A13_ . \n\nDefective dermatan sulfate biosynthesis can be detected by disaccharide analysis measured by anion-exchange chromatography in urine of patients with D4ST1-deficient \n\nor DS-epi1-deficient musculocontractural EDS. The lack of urinary dermatan sulfate disaccharides is specific for these conditions. \n\nWestern blot or enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) can detect reduced TN-X levels in classical-like EDS patients with biallelic variants in _TNXB_ . Nano-liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (nano-LC-MS/MS) can also determine serum TN-X levels. Spondylodysplastic EDS due to pathogenic variants in _B4GALT7_ and _B3GALT6_ is associated with significantly decreased serum levels of GAG-substituted bikunin and increased levels of GAG-free bikunin, a finding that can be quantified via Western blot. Although these Western blot, ELISA, and nano-LC-MS/MS analyses are sensitive enough to identify specific EDS types, they are not routinely available in a diagnostic setting. \n\n## **_Microscopic Studies of Skin Biopsies_** \n\nTransmission electron microscopy (TEM) can be performed on fixed skin biopsies in individuals with suspected EDS, allowing for ultrastructural analysis of collagen fibrils. Whereas dermal collagen fibrils are usually perfectly round on cross-section and are tightly packed, a range of alterations can be observed in EDS. While these alterations are not specific to EDS or an EDS subtype, their presence can support but not confirm an EDS diagnosis. \n\nLight microscopy and TEM of the dermis in individuals with suspected cutis laxa can be performed to classify the pathogenicity of a VUS in a cutis laxa–associated gene. Dermal elastic fibers are both altered and reduced in numbers; however, these changes may be difficult to appreciate on hematoxylin and eosin staining. TEM confirms irregular fragmentation of the elastic fiber structure and may help differentiate between different cutis laxa types.\n\n<!-- p.226 -->\n"});
